llowed by decarboxylation accounts for the experimentally reported D,L-threonine and allothreonine products (Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020). Standalone kinetics for this inferred intermediate under the modeled pool conditions are not supplied. Neither enantioselectivity nor a quantitative threonine:allothreonine ratio is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker supply of aminoacetonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K, proposed", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral to mildly alkaline concentrated water containing formaldehyde, ammonia, and cyanide; retain volatile feedstocks."}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde forms an imine/iminium species with ammonia; cyanide addition gives aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "Speculative upstream network integration relative to the supplied research, which does not separately document this glycine-supply experiment. The transformation is established general Strecker chemistry and is chemically analogous to the experimentally supported hydroxyaldehyde Strecker module of Ritson and Sutherland (2013). It supplies glycine without invoking the unsupported claim that generic aldehyde/ammonia mixtures directly yield threonine; Koga and Naraoka (2017) provide a negative constraint on that latter claim."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_022", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_023", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions for specific acid catalysis"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K, proposed", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidified water, approximately pH 1-3; environmental hydrolysis time is unspecified. Subsequent adjustment toward pH 3.3-3.7 enables the aldehyde/glycine stage."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis yield glycine and ammonia/ammonium.", "reasoning": "Speculative environmental supply module relative to the supplied studies. General aminonitrile hydrolysis supports the chemistry, but the cited threonine research does not establish this step's rate in the modeled pool. Its purpose is to provide the glycine substrate actually used by Ivanov and Ivanov (1983), while keeping the nitrile-hydrolysis bottleneck explicit."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Glycine-acetaldehyde threonine-forming reaction ensemble", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "333-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 3.3-3.7; homogeneous solution without inorganic solids. Requires sufficient glycine and acetaldehyde concentration and residence time."}, "mechanism": "Net C-C bond formation between the glycine alpha carbon and acetaldehyde gives the beta-hydroxy amino-acid skeleton. An imine-mediated aldol-like description is plausible, but the experimentally observed reaction network is not mechanistically resolved.", "reasoning": "Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) reported threonine among products of glycine/oligoglycine and aldehyde chemistry at these temperatures and pH, without inorganic solids. This supports a threonine-producing reaction ensemble, not a selective elementary aldol reaction. The supplied findings provide no isolated yield or stereoisomer ratio; the two listed threonine enantiomers express the achiral-model expectation, and omission of allo products here does not imply their absence."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Carbonyl-sulfide-mediated glycylglycine supply", "inputs": ["mol_023", "mol_027"], "outputs": ["mol_024", "mol_020", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K, proposed near-ambient window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", 