idence does not prove a unique intact-glycylglycine C-C-forming mechanism or distinguish it from chemistry following partial peptide hydrolysis. Consequently this defined peptide-fed sequence is a speculative mechanistic specialization of a supported reaction ensemble, not a proven independent elementary threonine pathway."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Bulk HCN-ammonia chemistry followed by hydrolysis: unresolved ensemble", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_020", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium ions during the subsequent hydrolysis stage"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K for oligomer formation; proposed environmental hydrolysis at 350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydronium ions during hydrolysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Concentrated alkaline/ammoniacal HCN first; subsequent acidic hydrolysis is a separate stage within this ensemble node. Historical analytical workups used strong acid, including approximately 6 M HCl near 373 K; a pH 1-3 natural replacement is unvalidated."}, "mechanism": "HCN oligomerization and rearrangement produce heterogeneous nitrogen-rich material that releases threonine on hydrolysis. No particular aminonitrile, peptide, or polymer structure is assigned to the unresolved residue.", "reasoning": "Hydrolysate threonine is supported by Matthews and Moser (1966, 1967), with related ammonium-cyanide complexity demonstrated by Lowe et al. (1963). Free threonine before workup was not established. Environmental completion is speculative. CO2 and released ammonia provide a possible atom-balanced disproportionation/hydrolysis projection; the studies do not establish this unique coproduct balance. Ammonia occurs on both sides because it is present initially and may also be liberated."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Aqueous cyanide radiolysis followed by hydrolysis: unresolved ensemble", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_0