m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Threonine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: A connected, target-focused network with 27 molecule nodes, 20 reaction nodes, and 10 distinct reaction sequences. The pathways comprise three alternative photoredox implementations of lactaldehyde/Strecker chemistry, aminomalononitrile chemistry, glycine-fed and oligoglycine-fed chemistry, bulk HCN chemistry, cyanide radiolysis, and two discharge configurations. These are not ten independently validated elementary mechanisms: implementation variants, unresolved reaction ensembles, and speculative environmental extensions are identified explicitly. L-threonine is the target node, but no route establishes biological stereoselectivity; D-threonine and, where supported or mechanistically expected, allothreonine are separate products. Historical reports lacking enantiomer-resolved measurements are represented by an expected racemic threonine pair, not a claimed measured ratio. All modeled environments are terrestrial Surface settings, without molecular oxygen or enzymes. Cryogenic extraterrestrial experiments are excluded rather than relabeled as terrestrial surface chemistry. Photochemistry occurs in illuminated water, whereas subsequent accumulation and hydrolysis require shielded or temporally separated conditions. 