istry, and distinguish reactor products from products released during workup.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_021\": 0.80\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.80,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.77,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.45,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.74,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.69,\n      \"feedback\": \"Matthews and Moser (1966) support threonine released by hydrolysis of residues generated in dry methane-ammonia discharge experiments. Including water in the overall equation is legitimate because the network explicitly assigns it to subsequent hydrolysis, not the dry discharge stage. The unresolved issues are environmental maintenance of the dry reducing gas mixture, residue collection and wetting, and hydrolysis sufficient to release free threonine.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_021\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Annotate the dry discharge, residue collection and aqueous hydrolysis as distinct stages within the ensemble.\",\n        \"Provide a physical setting and transport mechanism connecting residue formation to hydrolysis without assuming quantitative collection.\",\n        \"Measure absolute free-threonine recovery and product losses under environmentally justified hydrolysis conditions.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"best_pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n  \"overall_confidence\": 0.78,\n  \"network_feedback\": \"The network is structurally sound: its 25 molecule nodes and 21 reaction nodes form a connected graph, molecule IDs are consistent, and the deterministic check establishes zero conservation violations. Inferred coefficients, including sulfur multiples and coefficients for stereoisomer mixtures, are not yields or stereoisomer ratios. Listed problematic reactions concern evidence or environmental implementation, not atom or charge balance. Literature alignment is substantively stron