SR)-2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D,L-Threonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"(2RS,3RS)-2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"D,L-Allothreonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K, proposed surface operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, near-neutral water, proposed pH 7-8. Use approximately 10 mM acetaldehyde and comparable or excess total cyanide as a starting test condition, not a reported optimized recipe. Retain volatile feeds in a replenished or partially confined pool. Determine equilibrium conversion before irradiation; no formation time is supplied in the research summary.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl followed by proton transfer gives racemic lactonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the substrate-generating step of the experimentally supported acetaldehyde-to-lactaldehyde branch (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). No mineral is needed. Cyanide concentration and acetaldehyde loss must be measured rather than assumed; alkaline-lake cyanide storage provides a possible upstream concentration mechanism but does not establish the full local reagent inventory (Toner and Catling, 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-assisted sulfidic photoreduction of lactonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"CuCN-derived copper(I)-cyanide complexes, photoredox catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K, proposed temperature-controlled surface window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Published representative homologation conditions were 10 mM nitrile, 30 mM H2S, 33 mM phosphate, 10 mol% CuCN, pH 7, and 254 nm irradiation for 2 h. Those loadings were reported for a representative glycolonitrile experiment and are a starting benchmark here, not an asserted exact lactonitrile recipe. Lactaldehyde was reported at 19% after 2 h in the target-relevant experiment. Measure absorbed photon dose, dissolved sulfide, and copper speciation; laboratory irradiation time cannot be equated to sunlight exposure time.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox reduction of the nitrile to an imine-level intermediate followed by hydrolysis produces lactaldehyde and ammonia. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents; S8 represents the formal elemental-sulfur oxidation sink rather than a measured exclusive sulfur product.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lactaldehyde formation and the 19% endpoint are directly supported (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013), with broader network support from Patel et al. (2015). This is the principal experimentally defined carbon-skeleton bottleneck. Sulfide precipitation by abundant reduced metals and attenuation of UV by sulfur species can prevent the stated dissolved inventories from coexisting naturally (Ranjan et al., 2018). A shallow, optically characterized, locally supplied pool is required; its existence is not demonstrated by the laboratory yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K, proposed operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 7-8 with renewed cyanide supply. Transfer the photochemical product solution to a shaded receiving volume to limit further photochemistry. If a 10 mM lactonitrile experiment reproduced 19% lactaldehyde yield, the maximum undiluted lactaldehyde concentration would be about 1.9 mM before subsequent losses; this is a mass-balance illustration, not a measured concentration for the original experiment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanide attack on lactaldehyde gives its cyanohydrin as an unresolved stereoisomeric population.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The cyanohydrin is part of the reported lactaldehyde-to-threonine-aminonitrile sequence (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). Explicit representation prevents precursor production from being confused with amino-acid production. Lactaldehyde persistence against competing reactions remains unquantified, so immediate downstream trapping is a design requirement, not demonstrated natural preservation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia-driven conversion of lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K, proposed operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich, cyanide-containing water; proposed pH 8-9. Use ammonia in substantial excess over the millimolar carbonyl-derived pool; 0.05-0.2 M total ammonia is a screening range, not a published threonine-specific loading. Allow days to weeks while monitoring aminonitrile formation, cyanide loss, and competing products. Keep this stage separate from subsequent acid hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanohydrin dissociation replenishes lactaldehyde; ammonia forms an imine or iminium intermediate, and cyanide addition gives the alpha-aminonitrile. The net edge is not a claim of direct hydroxyl displacement.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formation of the threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile from lactaldehyde with HCN and excess ammonia was demonstrated (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). Days-to-weeks conversion times reported for related aldehydes motivate an explicit residence-time requirement, but do not establish an exact rate for this entire mixed surface solution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional hydration of threonine-family aminonitrile to amide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydrogen ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Transfer an aminonitrile-bearing aliquot to a separate warm acidic water body, proposed pH 1-2. Ammonia buffering capacity must first be exhausted or diluted; acidification is not assumed to occur spontaneously in the ammonia-rich reactor. Screen 1-30 days while measuring aminonitrile, amide, amino acid, and decomposition products. This interval is a proposed test budget, not a demonstrated conversion time.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-promoted water addition to the nitrile followed by proton transfers yields the corresponding carboxamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is an inferred hydrolysis substep, not an independently demonstrated endpoint of the threonine photoredox study (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). The supplied research establishes the aminonitrile much more securely than environmentally effective conversion to free threonine. The explicit amide node resolves the kinetic bottleneck without claiming that the intermediate was isolated. Natural completion at the proposed pH and temperature remains unvalidated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional amide hydrolysis releasing threonine and allothreonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydrogen ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Continue the separate pH 1-2 hydrolysis stage, sampling over the proposed 1-30 day interval. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions. Cool recovered amino-acid solution toward 300-320 K after conversion. Strong laboratory hydrolysis near 373 K is a comparison endpoint, not evidence that the milder modeled stage has equivalent recovery.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amide hydrolysis converts the carboxamide carbon into the carboxyl group and releases ammonia/ammonium. Existing stereoisomer populations furnish threonine and allothreonine; their proportions are unspecified.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The transformation is a chemically conventional but environmentally unvalidated completion of the aminonitrile route (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). Thanassi (1975) independently supports recovery of both threonine families after acid hydrolysis of a different nitrile precursor. Hydrolysis rates, amino-acid recovery, and concurrent degradation must be measured together; neither a quantitative yield nor stereochemical selection is inferred.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN oligomerization supplying aminomalononitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K, proposed concentrated-pool window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed 0.1-1 M total cyanide at pH 9-9.5 in a concentrated, shaded, anoxic reservoir. Ammonia/ammonium controls pH but is not consumed in the net trimerization. Sample over hours to days and divert material only when aminomalononitrile is analytically present. These concentrations and sampling times are proposed operating choices, not an optimized selective AMN preparation from the supplied studies.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-facilitated HCN addition and rearrangement generate the HCN trimer within a competing oligomerization network.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminomalononitrile is the established HCN trimer used in the threonine-forming electrophile reaction (Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020). Bulk HCN chemistry is supported by Lowe et al. (1963), but selective accumulation of this trimer is not guaranteed. Cyanide storage in alkaline lakes can help supply concentrated feedstocks without establishing the proposed AMN concentration or recovery (Toner and Catling, 2019).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminomalononitrile carbon-carbon addition to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K, proposed implementation of mild aqueous conditions\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Bring an AMN-containing fraction to approximately neutral pH, proposed pH 6.5-7.5, before acetaldehyde addition. Proposed initial reactant screening range: 10-100 mM each; actual recoverable AMN concentration may be much lower. The supplied report does not provide exact original loadings or incubation duration, so both must be experimentally established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Carbon-centered nucleophilic addition of the activated aminomalononitrile carbon to acetaldehyde gives the hydroxyethyl-substituted dinitrile connectivity.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thanassi (1975) demonstrated D,L-threonine and allothreonine after acetaldehyde reaction and hydrolytic workup; Kuroda et al. (2020) describes the carbon-carbon-forming stage as occurring under almost neutral aqueous conditions. No mineral catalyst is required. The depicted adduct is the mechanistic connectivity model for the hydrolyzable product, not a claim that the supplied abstract establishes its isolated yield or purity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of the acetaldehyde\u2013aminomalononitrile adduct\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydrogen ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Separate warm acidic stage, proposed pH 1-2. Follow nitrile disappearance, ammonium release, and amino-acid recovery over a proposed 1-30 day screening interval. Thanassi's endpoint involved laboratory acid hydrolysis; the exact acid loading, temperature, and duration are not supplied in the research summary and are not invented here.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of both nitrile groups through amide-level intermediates gives the substituted aminomalonic acid and ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The hydrolysis/decarboxylation sequence is supported at the threonine product level by Thanassi (1975), as discussed by Kuroda et al. (2020). The dicarboxylic acid is a mechanistic intermediate rather than a reported accumulated natural product. Efficient hydrolysis at the proposed environmental acidity is an open question, so direct laboratory access to threonine is distinguished from demonstrated environmental completion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Decarboxylation of hydroxyethylaminomalonic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K, proposed warm aqueous window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Retain the hydrolyzed material under acidic conditions, proposed pH 1-3, and monitor carbon dioxide and both threonine diastereomer families. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation can overlap rather than requiring isolation of the dicarboxylic acid. No separate decarboxylation rate or stereoisomer ratio is available from the supplied abstract.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Loss of one carboxyl group from the substituted aminomalonic acid followed by protonation generates the amino-acid alpha carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes the directly demonstrated AMN/acetaldehyde route to D,L-threonine and allothreonine (Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020). Neither selective threo production nor L-enantiomer enrichment is established. The assigned surface operating window is a proposal, whereas the reported experimental endpoint includes acid hydrolysis/decarboxylation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker synthesis of glycinonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K, proposed operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 8-9, 10-100 mM formaldehyde and total cyanide, and excess ammonia. Use a shaded aqueous collection volume and assay over hours to days rather than assigning an unsupported quantitative conversion. Competing cyanohydrin formation and HCN oligomerization must be included in material recovery.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde and ammonia form an imine-equivalent intermediate that is trapped by cyanide to give glycinonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is conventional Strecker chemistry and a chemically justified glycine-supply module consistent with the aminonitrile network discussed by Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). The supplied research does not provide a dedicated recipe or yield for this exact formaldehyde step; its integration with the downstream threonine reaction is a proposed extension, not a separately demonstrated complete pathway.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glycinonitrile to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydrogen ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Move the aminonitrile-bearing solution into a separate pH 1-2 warm acidic stage. Screen 1-30 days for glycine recovery and ammonia release. After hydrolysis, adjust the receiving solution to pH 3.3-3.7 and 333-353 K before the acetaldehyde condensation stage; the two pH regimes are not assumed simultaneous.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis yield glycine and ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a standard chemical completion of a Strecker glycine precursor, included to avoid treating glycine supply as automatic. The provided research supports aminonitrile-based prebiotic networks but supplies no environmental rate for this exact edge (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). Its proposed integration is conditional on recovery. The downstream glycine-to-threonine transformation has separate experimental support (Ivanov and Ivanov, 1983).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Warm weak-acid glycine\u2013acetaldehyde threonine-forming channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Published reaction regime: pH 3.3-3.7, 60-80 degrees C, homogeneous solution, and no inorganic solids. Proposed initial screening concentrations are 10-100 mM glycine and acetaldehyde, with sampling over 1-30 days; these concentrations and times are design choices because the supplied abstract does not specify them. Use a shaded warm acidic pool or covered pore-water volume with aldehyde replenishment. Do not assume selective conversion in mixed precursor solutions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net alpha-carbon addition of glycine to acetaldehyde installs the beta-hydroxyl and methyl-bearing carbon unit. Aldehyde-mediated imine/enamine chemistry is a mechanistic possibility, not an established unique elementary mechanism.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Threonine was identified in the broad glycine/oligoglycine\u2013aldehyde product network under these temperature and pH conditions without inorganic solids (Ivanov and Ivanov, 1983). The edge records its threonine-producing channel, not an exclusive product inventory or a known threonine:allothreonine ratio. Exact yield, rate, and performance in unpurified upstream mixtures remain unknown. This is not the unsupported claim that generic formaldehyde/acetaldehyde/ammonia mixtures necessarily make threonine; the negative result of Koga and Naraoka (2017) is respected.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Conditional hydrolysis of aminomalononitrile to aminomalonic acid\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydrogen ions\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Divert part of the AMN-containing solution before acetaldehyde addition. Acidify a separate receiving volume to proposed pH 1-2 and assay over a proposed 1-30 day interval. Aminomalonic acid need not accumulate if decarboxylation follows rapidly. No standalone AMN-to-glycine yield under this regime is supplied.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of the two nitriles gives aminomalonic acid with release of ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is an explicitly inferred glycine-supply extension of the dinitrile hydrolysis chemistry underlying Thanassi's AMN route, not a standalone experiment demonstrated in the supplied threonine report (Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020). It is chemically justified because the unsubstituted aminomalonic skeleton can decarboxylate to glycine, but precursor recovery and mild-environment kinetics require validation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminomalonic acid decarboxylation to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370 K, proposed operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic aqueous solution, proposed pH 1-3. Monitor glycine and carbon dioxide rather than imposing an unmeasured residence time. Before acetaldehyde coupling, condition the recovered solution to pH 3.3-3.7 and 333-353 K. Competition from residual HCN oligomers must be tested.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Decarboxylation of the aminomalonic acid skeleton followed by proton transfer gives glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the unsubstituted analogue of the hydrolysis/decarboxylation logic discussed for AMN-derived amino acids (Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020). The exact integrated AMN-to-glycine-to-threonine sequence is not demonstrated by those papers and is explicitly a mechanistic proposal. Glycine's subsequent target-forming reaction is supported independently by Ivanov and Ivanov (1983).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Thermal ammoniacal HCN ensemble: threonine-releasing channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen ions during the separate hydrolysis stage\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K precursor formation; 350-370 K proposed environmental hydrolysis\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis during hydrolysis only\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed precursor stage: 0.1-1 M total cyanide, ammonia-buffered pH 9-10, and days-to-weeks sampling. These are design windows, not a recovered exact historical recipe. Published amino-acid identification depended on strong analytical hydrolysis, including approximately 6 N HCl near 373 K for many hours in this research family. Proposed natural completion instead requires a separate pH 1-2 warm acidic stage; equivalent recovery there has not been demonstrated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN oligomerization and subsequent rearrangements produce chemically unresolved bound precursors; hydrolysis releases a threonine-containing fraction. This reaction node deliberately encompasses both stages without inventing a polymer molecular identity.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Historical HCN/ammonia hydrolysates contained threonine (Matthews and Moser, 1967; related work described in the supplied report), with broader ammonium-cyanide chemistry supported by Lowe et al. (1963). This establishes a laboratory product channel, not free-threonine accumulation in an alkaline pool. Ammonia on both sides represents the buffering reservoir and hydrolytic nitrogen release. Carbon dioxide is formal carbon/redox closure, not a reported threonine-specific coproduct yield. Cyanide concentration, precursor survival, and natural hydrolysis remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Thermal ammoniacal HCN ensemble: glycine-releasing channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Hydrogen ions during the separate hydrolysis stage\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K precursor formation; 350-370 K proposed environmental hydrolysis\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Acid catalysis during hydrolysis only\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use the same proposed concentrated ammoniacal HCN formation regime as rxn_016, but follow the glycine-releasing product fraction. Strong-acid laboratory workup is the evidentiary benchmark; pH 1-2 environmental release over a proposed 1-30 day screen is unvalidated. Condition the recovered glycine-bearing water to pH 3.3-3.7 and 333-353 K before supplying acetaldehyde.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"An unresolved HCN-derived precursor population releases glycine on hydrolysis. The edge does not assert that all glycine forms through aminomalononitrile or through a single polymer structure.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine occurs among products of ammonium-cyanide and HCN-polymer chemistry (Lowe et al., 1963; Matthews and Moser, 1967). Combining this observed glycine channel with the Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) reaction gives a chemically different carbon-skeleton route to threonine from the direct threonine-releasing channel. The two channel nodes describe alternative allocations within a complex ensemble and must not be summed as independently measured yields. Compatibility of the crude hydrolysate with acetaldehyde coupling remains untested.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous cyanide radiolysis ensemble: threonine-releasing channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K proposed irradiation window; 350-370 K proposed environmental hydrolysis\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Published irradiation feeds included 0.004-0.1 M HCN; the reported cobalt-60 dose rate was approximately 2 kGy per hour. Oxygen-free cyanide solutions were also studied independently. Total dose and the required dose-to-product curve are not supplied here. At the laboratory rate, exposure time t hours corresponds to 2t kGy; no natural dose rate is assumed. Subsequent hydrolysis is mandatory for the reported amino-acid endpoint. A proposed separate pH 1-2, 350-370 K environmental stage is not established as equivalent to analytical workup.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ionizing radiation generates water- and cyanide-derived reactive species, producing unresolved oligomeric precursors that release threonine upon hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Threonine was identified after irradiation and hydrolysis (Sweeney et al., 1976; Dragani\u0107 et al., 1977). The experiments support laboratory accessibility but not a quantified terrestrial source. A natural radionuclide inventory, absorbed dose rate, productive total dose, and competing destruction rate are all missing; cobalt-60 apparatus is not treated as a Hadean source. Carbon dioxide and ammonia provide formal ensemble closure rather than measured precursor structures or carbon partitioning.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous cyanide radiolysis ensemble: glycine-releasing channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K proposed irradiation window; 350-370 K proposed environmental hydrolysis\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Laboratory evidence uses 0.004-0.1 M HCN and approximately 2 kGy per hour irradiation, with subsequent hydrolysis. The supplied report does not provide the total dose required for a specified glycine yield. Keep irradiation separate from acetaldehyde coupling; recover a hydrolysate, remove it from continued irradiation, and condition it to pH 3.3-3.7 and 333-353 K. Neither a natural irradiation timescale nor recovery under mild environmental hydrolysis is known.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Radical-initiated cyanide chemistry generates hydrolyzable glycine precursors; hydrolysis supplies glycine for a subsequent, nonradiative acetaldehyde reaction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine was among the amino acids identified in the cyanide-radiolysis hydrolysates (Sweeney et al., 1976; Dragani\u0107 et al., 1977). Its downstream use is supported by a separate experiment (Ivanov and Ivanov, 1983), but the combined route has not been demonstrated. This branch avoids asserting that radiolysis directly installs threonine's hydroxyethyl unit; that unit is supplied later by acetaldehyde. The natural dosimetry and workup limitations remain explicit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"H2S-rich gas\u2013water electrical-discharge ensemble: threonine channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K proposed bulk gas\u2013water cycling window; transient discharge plasma is much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Reported initial non-water partial pressures sum to 695 mmHg; water vapor is additional\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reported 5 L apparatus charge: CH4 258 mmHg, H2S 100 mmHg, NH3 250 mmHg, and CO2 87 mmHg, with electrical discharge and gas\u2013water recirculation. Tungsten electrodes were present and sulfur deposition was observed; a catalytic role was not established. Exact electrical energy dose, discharge duration, temperature profile, and collection efficiency are not supplied in the research summary and are not invented. A local reducing plume feeding a cooler aqueous collector is the environmental analogue, not a globally uniform atmosphere.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Electrical discharge and subsequent gas\u2013water chemistry generate a complex organic ensemble containing threonine. The target-forming sequence and carbon-source allocation are unresolved; hydrogen and elemental sulfur are formal redox coproducts, not an exclusive measured output inventory.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Modern reanalysis identified threonine in the H2S-rich archived experiment (Parker et al., 2011). This is strong product-level evidence but does not specify an elementary pathway or time-resolved free-versus-bound inventory. No intentional mineral catalyst is justified. Environmental implementation requires a sufficiently reducing local gas supply, energy exposure, and aqueous capture; neither field production flux nor electrode-free reproduction is established by the supplied report.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"H2S-rich gas\u2013water electrical-discharge ensemble: glycine channel\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_021\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K proposed bulk gas\u2013water cycling window; 333-353 K for subsequent acetaldehyde coupling\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Reported initial non-water partial-pressure sum 695 mmHg; approximately ambient-pressure collection\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Use the reported CH4/H2S/NH3/CO2 partial pressures of 258/100/250/87 mmHg as the laboratory evidence anchor. Collect glycine-bearing aqueous material away from the discharge. Before rxn_013, reduce its ammonia buffering capacity and establish pH 3.3-3.7; add acetaldehyde only in that downstream stage. Transfer efficiency, glycine concentration, and mixed-product inhibition require measurement. No selective natural purification is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The discharge ensemble supplies a glycine-containing aqueous product fraction; subsequent acetaldehyde condensation, represented separately, constructs the threonine carbon skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine is among the amino acids in the archived discharge chemistry characterized by Parker et al. (2011), and glycine/acetaldehyde threonine formation has independent support (Ivanov and Ivanov, 1983). This composite route is distinct from direct discharge production of threonine, although its integrated operation has not been demonstrated. The same gas-composition, energy-dose, collection, and possible electrode/sulfur effects apply as in rxn_020; no quantitative natural yield is assigned.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-assisted lactaldehyde\u2013Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde is homologated through lactonitrile to lactaldehyde, trapped as a cyanohydrin, converted to the aminonitrile, and hydrolyzed to threonine/allothreonine. Lactaldehyde and aminonitrile formation are experimentally supported; completion under the proposed warm acidic environmental conditions remains conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\", \"mol_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminomalononitrile alkylation\u2013decarboxylation route\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN supplies aminomalononitrile; acetaldehyde installs the hydroxyethyl unit before dinitrile hydrolysis and decarboxylation. The target-forming laboratory sequence has direct threonine/allothreonine evidence. Selective upstream AMN recovery and replacement of strong laboratory hydrolysis by a natural stage are unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker glycine followed by acetaldehyde addition\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde, cyanide, and ammonia first supply glycine through glycinonitrile. Recovered glycine enters the experimentally supported warm weak-acid acetaldehyde reaction. This joins conventional precursor chemistry to a demonstrated target-forming channel, but the full staged sequence and recovery have not been measured.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminomalononitrile-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde addition\",\n      \"description\": \"The HCN trimer is hydrolyzed and decarboxylated before acetaldehyde addition, unlike P2, where acetaldehyde addition precedes hydrolysis. The AMN-to-glycine supply sequence is an explicit mechanistic extension requiring validation; the final glycine\u2013acetaldehyde channel has direct experimental support.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_020\", \"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct threonine release from thermally generated HCN-derived material\",\n      \"description\": \"Concentrated ammoniacal HCN chemistry generates unresolved precursors from which threonine is recovered by hydrolysis. This is a laboratory-established product channel represented as a multistage ensemble, not an identified molecular mechanism or demonstrated natural free-threonine source.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_016\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Thermal HCN-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde addition\",\n      \"description\": \"Follow the experimentally observed glycine-releasing fraction of thermal HCN chemistry, rather than its directly threonine-releasing fraction. After hydrolysis and pH/temperature conditioning, acetaldehyde supplies the remaining two-carbon unit. This composite pathway is experimentally motivated but not demonstrated end to end.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct threonine release from irradiated cyanide products\",\n      \"description\": \"Ionizing radiation generates cyanide-derived precursors that release threonine during subsequent hydrolysis. Laboratory evidence is direct at the post-hydrolysis product level; natural dose budgets, competing destruction, and environmental hydrolysis are unestablished.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_018\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Radiolytic glycine supply followed by nonradiative acetaldehyde addition\",\n      \"description\": \"Use the glycine-releasing fraction of irradiated cyanide products, stop irradiation, hydrolyze and recover the glycine-bearing material, then form threonine in a separate warm weak-acid stage. The two component chemistries are supported independently, while the integrated route and natural irradiation budget remain conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_019\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct threonine-producing H2S-rich discharge ensemble\",\n      \"description\": \"A strongly reducing CH4/NH3/CO2/H2S gas\u2013water discharge system produces a threonine-containing organic inventory. The laboratory endpoint is supported, but the mechanism, energy-normalized yield, electrode independence, and realistic local atmospheric supply are unresolved.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_020\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Discharge-derived glycine followed by warm acidic acetaldehyde addition\",\n      \"description\": \"Collect glycine from the reducing-gas discharge ensemble and subsequently construct threonine's hydroxyethyl-bearing skeleton through acetaldehyde addition. This differs from P9 by placing the target-forming carbon-carbon reaction in a downstream aqueous stage. Both component product channels are supported, but recovery, pH conditioning, and performance in the crude mixture require validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_021\", \"rxn_013\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_021\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_021\", \"mol_022\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_019\", \"rxn_021\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_022\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_016\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_020\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_023\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Threonine", "overall_strategy": "Construct 10 distinct, connected reaction sequences using six experimentally motivated reaction families: lactaldehyde/Strecker chemistry, aminomalononitrile alkylation, glycine\u2013acetaldehyde condensation, thermal HCN chemistry, cyanide radiolysis, and reducing-gas discharge chemistry. Three experimentally observed glycine-producing ensembles also supply separate downstream glycine\u2013acetaldehyde routes; these are distinct carbon-skeleton assembly sequences, not claims of ten independently demonstrated natural syntheses. Unsupported substrate-specific copper-free and ferrocyanide photoreduction variants, extraterrestrial-temperature experiments, and hot-vent synthesis are excluded. All modeled environments are terrestrial surface settings, with physically separate photochemical, concentrated alkaline, and warm acidic stages where required. Reaction conditions distinguish published observations from proposed environmental operating windows. Strong analytical hydrolysis is never treated as proof of equivalent natural hydrolysis. Several complete routes therefore remain conditional on precursor recovery and environmentally effective hydrolysis. The target node represents racemic threo-threonine, including L-threonine, rather than enantiopure biological material; allothreonine is a separate node where its formation is supported. Molecular formulas use neutral reference structures, with solution protonation controlled by the stated pH. Ensemble reactions describe selected product channels, not elementary mechanisms or complete product inventories; their inorganic coproducts provide formal elemental closure and are not measured product distributions. Inferred stoichiometric coefficients must not be interpreted as yields or stereoisomer ratios. The supplied evidence does not support numerical predictions of integrated natural production, recovery, or degradation.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H3N", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "Ethanal", "common_name": "Acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "Methane", "common_name": "Methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "Dihydrogen", "common_name": "Hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "Cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "Elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "Lactonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H6O2", "iupac_name": "2-Hydroxypropanal", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H7NO2", "iupac_name": "2,3-Dihydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "Threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C4H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanamide", "common_name": "Threoninamide/allothreoninamide", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C3H3N3", "iupac_name": "2-Aminopropanedinitrile", "common_name": "Aminomalononitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C5H7N3O", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-2-(1-hydroxyethyl)propanedinitrile", "common_name": "Aminomalononitrile\u2013acetaldehyde addition product", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C5H9NO5", "iupac_name": "2-Amino-2-(1-hydroxyethyl)propanedioic acid", "common_name": "Hydroxyethylaminomalonic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "C2H4N2", "iupac_name": "2-Aminoacetonitrile", "common_name": "Glycinonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C3H5NO4", "iupac_name": "2-Aminopropanedioic acid", "common_name": "Aminomalonic acid", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_021", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-Aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "Glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_022", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "(2RS,3SR)-2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "D,L-Threonine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_023", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "(2RS,3RS)-2-Amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "D,L-Allothreonine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K, proposed surface operating window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, near-neutral water, proposed pH 7-8. Use approximately 10 mM acetaldehyde and comparable or excess total cyanide as a starting test condition, not a reported optimized recipe. Retain volatile feeds in a replenished or partially confined pool. Determine equilibrium conversion before irradiation; no formation time is supplied in the research summary."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl followed by proton transfer gives racemic lactonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the substrate-generating step of the experimentally supported acetaldehyde-to-lactaldehyde branch (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). No mineral is needed. Cyanide concentration and acetaldehyde loss must be measured rather than assumed; alkaline-lake cyanide storage provides a possible upstream concentration mechanism but does not establish the full local reagent inventory (Toner and Catling, 2019)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Copper-assisted sulfidic photoreduction of lactonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CuCN-derived copper(I)-cyanide complexes, photoredox catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K, proposed temperature-controlled surface window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Copper(I)-cyanide photoredox system", "aqueous": true, "other": "Published representative homologation conditions were 10 mM nitrile, 30 mM H2S, 33 mM phosphate, 10 mol% CuCN, pH 7, and 254 nm irradiation for 2 h. Those loadings were reported for a representative glycolonitrile experiment and are a starting benchmark here, not an asserted exact lactonitrile recipe. Lactaldehyde was reported at 19% after 2 h in the target-relevant experiment. Measure absorbed photon dose, dissolved sulfide, and copper speciation; laboratory irradiation time cannot be equated to sunlight exposure time."}, "mechanism": "Photoredox reduction of the nitrile to an imine-level intermediate followed by hydrolysis produces lactaldehyde and ammonia. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents; S8 represents the formal elemental-sulfur oxidation sink rather than a measured exclusive sulfur product.", "reasoning": "Lactaldehyde formation and the 19% endpoint are directly supported (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013), with broader network support from Patel et al. (2015). This is the principal experimentally defined carbon-skeleton bottleneck. Sulfide precipitation by abundant reduced metals and attenuation of UV by sulfur species can prevent the stated dissolved inventories from coexisting naturally (Ranjan et al., 2018). A shallow, optically characterized, locally supplied pool is required; its existence is not demonstrated by the laboratory yield."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K, proposed operating window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 7-8 with renewed cyanide supply. Transfer the photochemical product solution to a shaded receiving volume to limit further photochemistry. If a 10 mM lactonitrile experiment reproduced 19% lactaldehyde yield, the maximum undiluted lactaldehyde concentration would be about 1.9 mM before subsequent losses; this is a mass-balance illustration, not a measured concentration for the original experiment."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanide attack on lactaldehyde gives its cyanohydrin as an unresolved stereoisomeric population.", "reasoning": "The cyanohydrin is part of the reported lactaldehyde-to-threonine-aminonitrile sequence (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). Explicit representation prevents precursor production from being confused with amino-acid production. Lactaldehyde persistence against competing reactions remains unquantified, so immediate downstream trapping is a design requirement, not demonstrated natural preservation."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Ammonia-driven conversion of lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K, proposed operating window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich, cyanide-containing water; proposed pH 8-9. Use ammonia in substantial excess over the millimolar carbonyl-derived pool; 0.05-0.2 M total ammonia is a screening range, not a published threonine-specific loading. Allow days to weeks while monitoring aminonitrile formation, cyanide loss, and competing products. Keep this stage separate from subsequent acid hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Cyanohydrin dissociation replenishes lactaldehyde; ammonia forms an imine or iminium intermediate, and cyanide addition gives the alpha-aminonitrile. The net edge is not a claim of direct hydroxyl displacement.", "reasoning": "Formation of the threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile from lactaldehyde with HCN and excess ammonia was demonstrated (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). Days-to-weeks conversion times reported for related aldehydes motivate an explicit residence-time requirement, but do not establish an exact rate for this entire mixed surface solution."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Conditional hydration of threonine-family aminonitrile to amide", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydrogen ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Transfer an aminonitrile-bearing aliquot to a separate warm acidic water body, proposed pH 1-2. Ammonia buffering capacity must first be exhausted or diluted; acidification is not assumed to occur spontaneously in the ammonia-rich reactor. Screen 1-30 days while measuring aminonitrile, amide, amino acid, and decomposition products. This interval is a proposed test budget, not a demonstrated conversion time."}, "mechanism": "Acid-promoted water addition to the nitrile followed by proton transfers yields the corresponding carboxamide.", "reasoning": "This is an inferred hydrolysis substep, not an independently demonstrated endpoint of the threonine photoredox study (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). The supplied research establishes the aminonitrile much more securely than environmentally effective conversion to free threonine. The explicit amide node resolves the kinetic bottleneck without claiming that the intermediate was isolated. Natural completion at the proposed pH and temperature remains unvalidated."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Conditional amide hydrolysis releasing threonine and allothreonine", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_023", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydrogen ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Continue the separate pH 1-2 hydrolysis stage, sampling over the proposed 1-30 day interval. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions. Cool recovered amino-acid solution toward 300-320 K after conversion. Strong laboratory hydrolysis near 373 K is a comparison endpoint, not evidence that the milder modeled stage has equivalent recovery."}, "mechanism": "Amide hydrolysis converts the carboxamide carbon into the carboxyl group and releases ammonia/ammonium. Existing stereoisomer populations furnish threonine and allothreonine; their proportions are unspecified.", "reasoning": "The transformation is a chemically conventional but environmentally unvalidated completion of the aminonitrile route (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013). Thanassi (1975) independently supports recovery of both threonine families after acid hydrolysis of a different nitrile precursor. Hydrolysis rates, amino-acid recovery, and concurrent degradation must be measured together; neither a quantitative yield nor stereochemical selection is inferred."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "HCN oligomerization supplying aminomalononitrile", "inputs": ["mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K, proposed concentrated-pool window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed 0.1-1 M total cyanide at pH 9-9.5 in a concentrated, shaded, anoxic reservoir. Ammonia/ammonium controls pH but is not consumed in the net trimerization. Sample over hours to days and divert material only when aminomalononitrile is analytically present. These concentrations and sampling times are proposed operating choices, not an optimized selective AMN preparation from the supplied studies."}, "mechanism": "Base-facilitated HCN addition and rearrangement generate the HCN trimer within a competing oligomerization network.", "reasoning": "Aminomalononitrile is the established HCN trimer used in the threonine-forming electrophile reaction (Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020). Bulk HCN chemistry is supported by Lowe et al. (1963), but selective accumulation of this trimer is not guaranteed. Cyanide storage in alkaline lakes can help supply concentrated feedstocks without establishing the proposed AMN concentration or recovery (Toner and Catling, 2019)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Aminomalononitrile carbon-carbon addition to acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K, proposed implementation of mild aqueous conditions", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Bring an AMN-containing fraction to approximately neutral pH, proposed pH 6.5-7.5, before acetaldehyde addition. Proposed initial reactant screening range: 10-100 mM each; actual recoverable AMN concentration may be much lower. The supplied report does not provide exact original loadings or incubation duration, so both must be experimentally established."}, "mechanism": "Carbon-centered nucleophilic addition of the activated aminomalononitrile carbon to acetaldehyde gives the hydroxyethyl-substituted dinitrile connectivity.", "reasoning": "Thanassi (1975) demonstrated D,L-threonine and allothreonine after acetaldehyde reaction and hydrolytic workup; Kuroda et al. (2020) describes the carbon-carbon-forming stage as occurring under almost neutral aqueous conditions. No mineral catalyst is required. The depicted adduct is the mechanistic connectivity model for the hydrolyzable product, not a claim that the supplied abstract establishes its isolated yield or purity."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydrolysis of the acetaldehyde\u2013aminomalononitrile adduct", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_018", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydrogen ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Separate warm acidic stage, proposed pH 1-2. Follow nitrile disappearance, ammonium release, and amino-acid recovery over a proposed 1-30 day screening interval. Thanassi's endpoint involved laboratory acid hydrolysis; the exact acid loading, temperature, and duration are not supplied in the research summary and are not invented here."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of both nitrile groups through amide-level intermediates gives the substituted aminomalonic acid and ammonia/ammonium.", "reasoning": "The hydrolysis/decarboxylation sequence is supported at the threonine product level by Thanassi (1975), as discussed by Kuroda et al. (2020). The dicarboxylic acid is a mechanistic intermediate rather than a reported accumulated natural product. Efficient hydrolysis at the proposed environmental acidity is an open question, so direct laboratory access to threonine is distinguished from demonstrated environmental completion."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Decarboxylation of hydroxyethylaminomalonic acid", "inputs": ["mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_023", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K, proposed warm aqueous window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Retain the hydrolyzed material under acidic conditions, proposed pH 1-3, and monitor carbon dioxide and both threonine diastereomer families. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation can overlap rather than requiring isolation of the dicarboxylic acid. No separate decarboxylation rate or stereoisomer ratio is available from the supplied abstract."}, "mechanism": "Loss of one carboxyl group from the substituted aminomalonic acid followed by protonation generates the amino-acid alpha carbon.", "reasoning": "This completes the directly demonstrated AMN/acetaldehyde route to D,L-threonine and allothreonine (Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020). Neither selective threo production nor L-enantiomer enrichment is established. The assigned surface operating window is a proposal, whereas the reported experimental endpoint includes acid hydrolysis/decarboxylation."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker synthesis of glycinonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K, proposed operating window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 8-9, 10-100 mM formaldehyde and total cyanide, and excess ammonia. Use a shaded aqueous collection volume and assay over hours to days rather than assigning an unsupported quantitative conversion. Competing cyanohydrin formation and HCN oligomerization must be included in material recovery."}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde and ammonia form an imine-equivalent intermediate that is trapped by cyanide to give glycinonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is conventional Strecker chemistry and a chemically justified glycine-supply module consistent with the aminonitrile network discussed by Ritson and Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). The supplied research does not provide a dedicated recipe or yield for this exact formaldehyde step; its integration with the downstream threonine reaction is a proposed extension, not a separately demonstrated complete pathway."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydrolysis of glycinonitrile to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydrogen ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Move the aminonitrile-bearing solution into a separate pH 1-2 warm acidic stage. Screen 1-30 days for glycine recovery and ammonia release. After hydrolysis, adjust the receiving solution to pH 3.3-3.7 and 333-353 K before the acetaldehyde condensation stage; the two pH regimes are not assumed simultaneous."}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis yield glycine and ammonia/ammonium.", "reasoning": "This is a standard chemical completion of a Strecker glycine precursor, included to avoid treating glycine supply as automatic. The provided research supports aminonitrile-based prebiotic networks but supplies no environmental rate for this exact edge (Ritson and Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). Its proposed integration is conditional on recovery. The downstream glycine-to-threonine transformation has separate experimental support (Ivanov and Ivanov, 1983)."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Warm weak-acid glycine\u2013acetaldehyde threonine-forming channel", "inputs": ["mol_021", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_022"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "333-353 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Published reaction regime: pH 3.3-3.7, 60-80 degrees C, homogeneous solution, and no inorganic solids. Proposed initial screening concentrations are 10-100 mM glycine and acetaldehyde, with sampling over 1-30 days; these concentrations and times are design choices because the supplied abstract does not specify them. Use a shaded warm acidic pool or covered pore-water volume with aldehyde replenishment. Do not assume selective conversion in mixed precursor solutions."}, "mechanism": "Net alpha-carbon addition of glycine to acetaldehyde installs the beta-hydroxyl and methyl-bearing carbon unit. Aldehyde-mediated imine/enamine chemistry is a mechanistic possibility, not an established unique elementary mechanism.", "reasoning": "Threonine was identified in the broad glycine/oligoglycine\u2013aldehyde product network under these temperature and pH conditions without inorganic solids (Ivanov and Ivanov, 1983). The edge records its threonine-producing channel, not an exclusive product inventory or a known threonine:allothreonine ratio. Exact yield, rate, and performance in unpurified upstream mixtures remain unknown. This is not the unsupported claim that generic formaldehyde/acetaldehyde/ammonia mixtures necessarily make threonine; the negative result of Koga and Naraoka (2017) is respected."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Conditional hydrolysis of aminomalononitrile to aminomalonic acid", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions, acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K, proposed environmental hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Hydrogen ions", "aqueous": true, "other": "Divert part of the AMN-containing solution before acetaldehyde addition. Acidify a separate receiving volume to proposed pH 1-2 and assay over a proposed 1-30 day interval. Aminomalonic acid need not accumulate if decarboxylation follows rapidly. No standalone AMN-to-glycine yield under this regime is supplied."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis of the two nitriles gives aminomalonic acid with release of ammonia/ammonium.", "reasoning": "This is an explicitly inferred glycine-supply extension of the dinitrile hydrolysis chemistry underlying Thanassi's AMN route, not a standalone experiment demonstrated in the supplied threonine report (Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020). It is chemically justified because the unsubstituted aminomalonic skeleton can decarboxylate to glycine, but precursor recovery and mild-environment kinetics require validation."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Aminomalonic acid decarboxylation to glycine", "inputs": ["mol_020"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370 K, proposed operating window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic aqueous solution, proposed pH 1-3. Monitor glycine and carbon dioxide rather than imposing an unmeasured residence time. Before acetaldehyde coupling, condition the recovered solution to pH 3.3-3.7 and 333-353 K. Competition from residual HCN oligomers must be tested."}, "mechanism": "Decarboxylation of the aminomalonic acid skeleton followed by proton transfer gives glycine.", "reasoning": "This is the unsubstituted analogue of the hydrolysis/decarboxylation logic discussed for AMN-derived amino acids (Thanassi, 1975; Kuroda et al., 2020). The exact integrated AMN-to-glycine-to-threonine sequence is not demonstrated by those papers and is explicitly a mechanistic proposal. Glycine's subsequent target-forming reaction is supported independently by Ivanov and Ivanov (1983)."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Thermal ammoniacal HCN ensemble: threonine-releasing channel", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_002", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions during the separate hydrolysis stage"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K precursor formation; 350-370 K proposed environmental hydrolysis", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis during hydrolysis only", "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed precursor stage: 0.1-1 M total cyanide, ammonia-buffered pH 9-10, and days-to-weeks sampling. These are design windows, not a recovered exact historical recipe. Published amino-acid identification depended on strong analytical hydrolysis, including approximately 6 N HCl near 373 K for many hours in this research family. Proposed natural completion instead requires a separate pH 1-2 warm acidic stage; equivalent recovery there has not been demonstrated."}, "mechanism": "HCN oligomerization and subsequent rearrangements produce chemically unresolved bound precursors; hydrolysis releases a threonine-containing fraction. This reaction node deliberately encompasses both stages without inventing a polymer molecular identity.", "reasoning": "Historical HCN/ammonia hydrolysates contained threonine (Matthews and Moser, 1967; related work described in the supplied report), with broader ammonium-cyanide chemistry supported by Lowe et al. (1963). This establishes a laboratory product channel, not free-threonine accumulation in an alkaline pool. Ammonia on both sides represents the buffering reservoir and hydrolytic nitrogen release. Carbon dioxide is formal carbon/redox closure, not a reported threonine-specific coproduct yield. Cyanide concentration, precursor survival, and natural hydrolysis remain unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_017", "name": "Thermal ammoniacal HCN ensemble: glycine-releasing channel", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_002", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydrogen ions during the separate hydrolysis stage"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K precursor formation; 350-370 K proposed environmental hydrolysis", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Acid catalysis during hydrolysis only", "aqueous": true, "other": "Use the same proposed concentrated ammoniacal HCN formation regime as rxn_016, but follow the glycine-releasing product fraction. Strong-acid laboratory workup is the evidentiary benchmark; pH 1-2 environmental release over a proposed 1-30 day screen is unvalidated. Condition the recovered glycine-bearing water to pH 3.3-3.7 and 333-353 K before supplying acetaldehyde."}, "mechanism": "An unresolved HCN-derived precursor population releases glycine on hydrolysis. The edge does not assert that all glycine forms through aminomalononitrile or through a single polymer structure.", "reasoning": "Glycine occurs among products of ammonium-cyanide and HCN-polymer chemistry (Lowe et al., 1963; Matthews and Moser, 1967). Combining this observed glycine channel with the Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) reaction gives a chemically different carbon-skeleton route to threonine from the direct threonine-releasing channel. The two channel nodes describe alternative allocations within a complex ensemble and must not be summed as independently measured yields. Compatibility of the crude hydrolysate with acetaldehyde coupling remains untested."}, {"id": "rxn_018", "name": "Aqueous cyanide radiolysis ensemble: threonine-releasing channel", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_002", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K proposed irradiation window; 350-370 K proposed environmental hydrolysis", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Published irradiation feeds included 0.004-0.1 M HCN; the reported cobalt-60 dose rate was approximately 2 kGy per hour. Oxygen-free cyanide solutions were also studied independently. Total dose and the required dose-to-product curve are not supplied here. At the laboratory rate, exposure time t hours corresponds to 2t kGy; no natural dose rate is assumed. Subsequent hydrolysis is mandatory for the reported amino-acid endpoint. A proposed separate pH 1-2, 350-370 K environmental stage is not established as equivalent to analytical workup."}, "mechanism": "Ionizing radiation generates water- and cyanide-derived reactive species, producing unresolved oligomeric precursors that release threonine upon hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Threonine was identified after irradiation and hydrolysis (Sweeney et al., 1976; Dragani\u0107 et al., 1977). The experiments support laboratory accessibility but not a quantified terrestrial source. A natural radionuclide inventory, absorbed dose rate, productive total dose, and competing destruction rate are all missing; cobalt-60 apparatus is not treated as a Hadean source. Carbon dioxide and ammonia provide formal ensemble closure rather than measured precursor structures or carbon partitioning."}, {"id": "rxn_019", "name": "Aqueous cyanide radiolysis ensemble: glycine-releasing channel", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_002", "mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K proposed irradiation window; 350-370 K proposed environmental hydrolysis", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Laboratory evidence uses 0.004-0.1 M HCN and approximately 2 kGy per hour irradiation, with subsequent hydrolysis. The supplied report does not provide the total dose required for a specified glycine yield. Keep irradiation separate from acetaldehyde coupling; recover a hydrolysate, remove it from continued irradiation, and condition it to pH 3.3-3.7 and 333-353 K. Neither a natural irradiation timescale nor recovery under mild environmental hydrolysis is known."}, "mechanism": "Radical-initiated cyanide chemistry generates hydrolyzable glycine precursors; hydrolysis supplies glycine for a subsequent, nonradiative acetaldehyde reaction.", "reasoning": "Glycine was among the amino acids identified in the cyanide-radiolysis hydrolysates (Sweeney et al., 1976; Dragani\u0107 et al., 1977). Its downstream use is supported by a separate experiment (Ivanov and Ivanov, 1983), but the combined route has not been demonstrated. This branch avoids asserting that radiolysis directly installs threonine's hydroxyethyl unit; that unit is supplied later by acetaldehyde. The natural dosimetry and workup limitations remain explicit."}, {"id": "rxn_020", "name": "H2S-rich gas\u2013water electrical-discharge ensemble: threonine channel", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002", "mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_009", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K proposed bulk gas\u2013water cycling window; transient discharge plasma is much hotter", "pressure": "Reported initial non-water partial pressures sum to 695 mmHg; water vapor is additional", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reported 5 L apparatus charge: CH4 258 mmHg, H2S 100 mmHg, NH3 250 mmHg, and CO2 87 mmHg, with electrical discharge and gas\u2013water recirculation. Tungsten electrodes were present and sulfur deposition was observed; a catalytic role was not established. Exact electrical energy dose, discharge duration, temperature profile, and collection efficiency are not supplied in the research summary and are not invented. A local reducing plume feeding a cooler aqueous collector is the environmental analogue, not a globally uniform atmosphere."}, "mechanism": "Electrical discharge and subsequent gas\u2013water chemistry generate a complex organic ensemble containing threonine. The target-forming sequence and carbon-source allocation are unresolved; hydrogen and elemental sulfur are formal redox coproducts, not an exclusive measured output inventory.", "reasoning": "Modern reanalysis identified threonine in the H2S-rich archived experiment (Parker et al., 2011). This is strong product-level evidence but does not specify an elementary pathway or time-resolved free-versus-bound inventory. No intentional mineral catalyst is justified. Environmental implementation requires a sufficiently reducing local gas supply, energy exposure, and aqueous capture; neither field production flux nor electrode-free reproduction is established by the supplied report."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "H2S-rich gas\u2013water electrical-discharge ensemble: glycine channel", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002", "mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_021", "mol_009", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K proposed bulk gas\u2013water cycling window; 333-353 K for subsequent acetaldehyde coupling", "pressure": "Reported initial non-water partial-pressure sum 695 mmHg; approximately ambient-pressure collection", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Use the reported CH4/H2S/NH3/CO2 partial pressures of 258/100/250/87 mmHg as the laboratory evidence anchor. Collect glycine-bearing aqueous material away from the discharge. Before rxn_013, reduce its ammonia buffering capacity and establish pH 3.3-3.7; add acetaldehyde only in that downstream stage. Transfer efficiency, glycine concentration, and mixed-product inhibition require measurement. No selective natural purification is assumed."}, "mechanism": "The discharge ensemble supplies a glycine-containing aqueous product fraction; subsequent acetaldehyde condensation, represented separately, constructs the threonine carbon skeleton.", "reasoning": "Glycine is among the amino acids in the archived discharge chemistry characterized by Parker et al. (2011), and glycine/acetaldehyde threonine formation has independent support (Ivanov and Ivanov, 1983). This composite route is distinct from direct discharge production of threonine, although its integrated operation has not been demonstrated. The same gas-composition, energy-dose, collection, and possible electrode/sulfur effects apply as in rxn_020; no quantitative natural yield is assigned."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Copper-assisted lactaldehyde\u2013Strecker route", "description": "Acetaldehyde is homologated through lactonitrile to lactaldehyde, trapped as a cyanohydrin, converted to the aminonitrile, and hydrolyzed to threonine/allothreonine. Lactaldehyde and aminonitrile formation are experimentally supported; completion under the proposed warm acidic environmental conditions remains conditional.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_015"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Aminomalononitrile alkylation\u2013decarboxylation route", "description": "HCN supplies aminomalononitrile; acetaldehyde installs the hydroxyethyl unit before dinitrile hydrolysis and decarboxylation. The target-forming laboratory sequence has direct threonine/allothreonine evidence. Selective upstream AMN recovery and replacement of strong laboratory hydrolysis by a natural stage are unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_018"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker glycine followed by acetaldehyde addition", "description": "Formaldehyde, cyanide, and ammonia first supply glycine through glycinonitrile. Recovered glycine enters the experimentally supported warm weak-acid acetaldehyde reaction. This joins conventional precursor chemistry to a demonstrated target-forming channel, but the full staged sequence and recovery have not been measured.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_019", "mol_021"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Aminomalononitrile-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde addition", "description": "The HCN trimer is hydrolyzed and decarboxylated before acetaldehyde addition, unlike P2, where acetaldehyde addition precedes hydrolysis. The AMN-to-glycine supply sequence is an explicit mechanistic extension requiring validation; the final glycine\u2013acetaldehyde channel has direct experimental support.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_014", "rxn_015", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_016", "mol_020", "mol_021"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Direct threonine release from thermally generated HCN-derived material", "description": "Concentrated ammoniacal HCN chemistry generates unresolved precursors from which threonine is recovered by hydrolysis. This is a laboratory-established product channel represented as a multistage ensemble, not an identified molecular mechanism or demonstrated natural free-threonine source.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_016"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Thermal HCN-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde addition", "description": "Follow the experimentally observed glycine-releasing fraction of thermal HCN chemistry, rather than its directly threonine-releasing fraction. After hydrolysis and pH/temperature conditioning, acetaldehyde supplies the remaining two-carbon unit. This composite pathway is experimentally motivated but not demonstrated end to end.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_017", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_021"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Direct threonine release from irradiated cyanide products", "description": "Ionizing radiation generates cyanide-derived precursors that release threonine during subsequent hydrolysis. Laboratory evidence is direct at the post-hydrolysis product level; natural dose budgets, competing destruction, and environmental hydrolysis are unestablished.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_018"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Radiolytic glycine supply followed by nonradiative acetaldehyde addition", "description": "Use the glycine-releasing fraction of irradiated cyanide products, stop irradiation, hydrolyze and recover the glycine-bearing material, then form threonine in a separate warm weak-acid stage. The two component chemistries are supported independently, while the integrated route and natural irradiation budget remain conditional.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_019", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_021"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Direct threonine-producing H2S-rich discharge ensemble", "description": "A strongly reducing CH4/NH3/CO2/H2S gas\u2013water discharge system produces a threonine-containing organic inventory. The laboratory endpoint is supported, but the mechanism, energy-normalized yield, electrode independence, and realistic local atmospheric supply are unresolved.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_020"], "key_intermediates": []}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Discharge-derived glycine followed by warm acidic acetaldehyde addition", "description": "Collect glycine from the reducing-gas discharge ensemble and subsequently construct threonine's hydroxyethyl-bearing skeleton through acetaldehyde addition. This differs from P9 by placing the target-forming carbon-carbon reaction in a downstream aqueous stage. Both component product channels are supported, but recovery, pH conditioning, and performance in the crude mixture require validation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_021", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_021"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_004", "mol_016", "mol_021", "mol_022"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_004", "incoming_reactions": [], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_008", "rxn_013"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_016", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_007"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_008", "rxn_014"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_021", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_012", "rxn_015", "rxn_017", "rxn_019", "rxn_021"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_013"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_022", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_010", "rxn_013", "rxn_016", "rxn_018", "rxn_020"], "outgoing_reactions": []}, {"molecule_id": "mol_023", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_010"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 411207.4239253998, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T14:20:31.151104", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Threonine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct 10 distinct, connected reaction sequences using six experimentally motivated reaction families: lactaldehyde/Strecker chemistry, aminomalononitrile alkylation, glycine\u2013acetaldehyde condensation, thermal HCN chemistry, cyanide radiolysis, and reducing-gas discharge chemistry. Three experimentally observed glycine-producing ensembles also supply separate downstream glycine\u2013acetaldehyde routes; these are distinct carbon-skeleton assembly sequences, not claims of ten independently demonstrated natural syntheses. Unsupported substrate-specific copper-free and ferrocyanide photoreduction variants, extraterrestrial-temperature experiments, and hot-vent synthesis are excluded. All modeled environments are terrestrial surface settings, with physically separate photochemical, concentrated alkaline, and warm acidic stages where required. Reaction conditions distinguish published observations from proposed environmental operating windows. Strong analytical hydrolysis is never treated as proof of equivalent natural hydrolysis. Several complete routes therefore remain conditional on precursor recovery and environmentally effective hydrolysis. The target node represents racemic threo-threonine, including L-threonine, rather than enantiopure biological material; allothreonine is a separate node where its formation is supported. Molecular formulas use neutral reference structures, with solution protonation controlled by the stated pH. Ensemble reactions describe selected product channels, not elementary mechanisms or complete product inventories; their inorganic coproducts provide formal elemental closure and are not measured product distributions. Inferred stoichiometric coefficients must not be interpreted as yields or stereoisomer ratios. The supplied evidence does not support numerical predictions of integrated natural production, recovery, or degradation.\n\nMOLECULES (23 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CHN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H3N (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2O (Water) [starting]\n  - mol_004: C2H4O (Acetaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2S (Hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_006: CH2O (Formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_007: CH4 (Methane) [starting]\n  - mol_008: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_009: H2 (Hydrogen) [intermediate]\n  - mol_010: S8 (Elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_011: C3H5NO (Lactonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C3H6O2 (Lactaldehyde) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C4H7NO2 (Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C4H8N2O (Threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C4H10N2O2 (Threoninamide/allothreoninamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C3H3N3 (Aminomalononitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C5H7N3O (Aminomalononitrile\u2013acetaldehyde addition product) [intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C5H9NO5 (Hydroxyethylaminomalonic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: C2H4N2 (Glycinonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C3H5NO4 (Aminomalonic acid) [intermediate]\n  - mol_021: C2H5NO2 (Glycine) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_022: C4H9NO3 (D,L-Threonine) [target]\n  - mol_023: C4H9NO3 (D,L-Allothreonine) [intermediate]\n\nREACTIONS (21 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_004, mol_001 -> mol_011 (Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_011, mol_005, mol_003 -> mol_012, mol_002, mol_010 (Copper-assisted sulfidic photoreduction of lactonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_012, mol_001 -> mol_013 (Lactaldehyde cyanohydrin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_013, mol_002 -> mol_014, mol_003 (Ammonia-driven conversion of lactaldehyde cyanohydrin to aminonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_014, mol_003 -> mol_015 (Conditional hydration of threonine-family aminonitrile to amide) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_015, mol_003 -> mol_022, mol_023, mol_002 (Conditional amide hydrolysis releasing threonine and allothreonine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_007: mol_001 -> mol_016 (HCN oligomerization supplying aminomalononitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_016, mol_004 -> mol_017 (Aminomalononitrile carbon-carbon addition to acetaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_017, mol_003 -> mol_018, mol_002 (Hydrolysis of the acetaldehyde\u2013aminomalononitrile adduct) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_018 -> mol_022, mol_023, mol_008 (Decarboxylation of hydroxyethylaminomalonic acid) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_006, mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_019, mol_003 (Formaldehyde Strecker synthesis of glycinonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_019, mol_003 -> mol_021, mol_002 (Hydrolysis of glycinonitrile to glycine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_013: mol_021, mol_004 -> mol_022 (Warm weak-acid glycine\u2013acetaldehyde threonine-forming channel) [Surface]\n  - rxn_014: mol_016, mol_003 -> mol_020, mol_002 (Conditional hydrolysis of aminomalononitrile to aminomalonic acid) [Surface]\n  - rxn_015: mol_020 -> mol_021, mol_008 (Aminomalonic acid decarboxylation to glycine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_016: mol_001, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_022, mol_002, mol_008 (Thermal ammoniacal HCN ensemble: threonine-releasing channel) [Surface]\n  - rxn_017: mol_001, mol_002, mol_003 -> mol_021, mol_002, mol_008 (Thermal ammoniacal HCN ensemble: glycine-releasing channel) [Surface]\n  - rxn_018: mol_001, mol_003 -> mol_022, mol_002, mol_008 (Aqueous cyanide radiolysis ensemble: threonine-releasing channel) [Surface]\n  - rxn_019: mol_001, mol_003 -> mol_021, mol_002, mol_008 (Aqueous cyanide radiolysis ensemble: glycine-releasing channel) [Surface]\n  - rxn_020: mol_007, mol_002, mol_008, mol_005, mol_003 -> mol_022, mol_009, mol_010 (H2S-rich gas\u2013water electrical-discharge ensemble: threonine channel) [Surface]\n  - rxn_021: mol_007, mol_002, mol_008, mol_005, mol_003 -> mol_021, mol_009, mol_010 (H2S-rich gas\u2013water electrical-discharge ensemble: glycine channel) [Surface]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Copper-assisted lactaldehyde\u2013Strecker route (6 steps)\n  - P2: Aminomalononitrile alkylation\u2013decarboxylation route (4 steps)\n  - P3: Formaldehyde Strecker glycine followed by acetaldehyde addition (3 steps)\n  - P4: Aminomalononitrile-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde addition (4 steps)\n  - P5: Direct threonine release from thermally generated HCN-derived material (1 steps)\n  - P6: Thermal HCN-derived glycine followed by acetaldehyde addition (2 steps)\n  - P7: Direct threonine release from irradiated cyanide products (1 steps)\n  - P8: Radiolytic glycine supply followed by nonradiative acetaldehyde addition (2 steps)\n  - P9: Direct threonine-producing H2S-rich discharge ensemble (1 steps)\n  - P10: Discharge-derived glycine followed by warm acidic acetaldehyde addition (2 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 21 reactions | 8 balanced as written | 13 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_002 balanceable: 8 C3H5NO + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 C3H6O2 + 8 H3N + S8\n  - rxn_006 balanceable: 2 C4H10N2O2 + 2 H2O -> C4H9NO3 + C4H9NO3 + 2 H3N\n  - rxn_007 balanceable: 3 CHN -> C3H3N3\n  - rxn_009 balanceable: C5H7N3O + 4 H2O -> C5H9NO5 + 2 H3N\n  - rxn_010 balanceable: 2 C5H9NO5 -> C4H9NO3 + C4H9NO3 + 2 CO2\n  - rxn_012 balanceable: C2H4N2 + 2 H2O -> C2H5NO2 + H3N\n  - rxn_014 balanceable: C3H3N3 + 4 H2O -> C3H5NO4 + 2 H3N\n  - rxn_016 balanceable: 8 CHN + 8 H3N + 11 H2O -> C4H9NO3 + 8 H3N + 4 CO2\n  - rxn_017 balanceable: 3 CHN + 3 H3N + 4 H2O -> C2H5NO2 + 3 H3N + CO2\n  - rxn_018 balanceable: 8 CHN + 11 H2O -> C4H9NO3 + 7 H3N + 4 CO2\n  - rxn_019 balanceable: 3 CHN + 4 H2O -> C2H5NO2 + 2 H3N + CO2\n  - rxn_020 balanceable: 3 CH4 + H3N + CO2 + 8 H2S + H2O -> C4H9NO3 + 12 H2 + S8\n  - rxn_021 balanceable: 3 CH4 + 2 H3N + CO2 + 8 H2S + 2 H2O -> 2 C2H5NO2 + 14 H2 + S8\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of Threonine: Routes, Intermediates, Catalysts, Environments, and Unresolved Problems\n\n## Scope and overall assessment\n\nThreonine is a comparatively difficult proteinogenic amino acid to account for prebiotically because its four-carbon skeleton contains **two adjacent stereogenic centers and a \u03b2-hydroxyl group**. The literature nevertheless contains several genuinely distinct routes to threonine or to chemically immediate threonine precursors. They span aqueous HCN chemistry, Strecker chemistry, aldehyde\u2013amino-acid condensation, atmospheric electrical discharge, ionizing-radiation chemistry, and extraterrestrial ice photochemistry. The evidentiary strength is very uneven: some experiments isolated or chromatographically identified threonine after hydrolysis, whereas others stopped at lactaldehyde or the threonine aminonitrile and therefore established a **precursor route rather than free threonine itself**.\n\nThe strongest threonine-specific conclusions from the primary literature are:\n\n| Route class | What has actually been demonstrated? | Main feedstocks | Evidentiary status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Cyanosulfidic/Kiliani\u2013Fischer\u2013Strecker sequence | Lactaldehyde in **19% yield**, followed by conversion to the threonine/allo-threonine aminonitrile | HCN-derived nitriles, H\u2082S, NH\u2083, UV; Cu(I) in the original implementation | **Strong precursor-level demonstration**; free-threonine hydrolysis was not the central endpoint of the experiment. |\n| Aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde | D,L-threonine and allo-threonine after hydrolysis/decarboxylation | HCN trimer + CH\u2083CHO | **Direct experimental threonine route** under mild aqueous conditions. |\n| Glycine/oligoglycine + acetaldehyde | Threonine among products | Glycine or glycine oligomers + CH\u2083CHO | **Direct experimental route**, catalyst-free, pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C, but poorly selective. |\n| Concentrated HCN/NH\u2083 polymer chemistry | Threonine released from hydrolyzed HCN-derived material | HCN or NH\u2083/HCN systems | **Historical direct detection after strong hydrolysis**; chemical identity of the immediate precursor is poorly constrained. |\n| \u03b3-radiolysis of aqueous HCN/cyanide | Threonine after hydrolysis of irradiated material | HCN, or NH\u2084CN/NaCN + ionizing radiation | **Direct post-hydrolysis detection**, independently reproduced in the 1970s. |\n| Electric-discharge chemistry | Threonine in the H\u2082S-rich 1958 Miller experiment | CH\u2084, NH\u2083, CO\u2082, H\u2082S, H\u2082O + spark | **Direct product-level detection**, but pathway and environmental realism are uncertain. |\n| UV photolysis of interstellar ice analogues | Threonine and allothreonine after ice photochemistry/workup | H\u2082O, C\u2082H\u2085OH, HCN, NH\u2083 | **Direct laboratory production**, with Thr:Gly = 60:100 in one ethanol-ice experiment. |\n| PAH-containing astrophysical ice | Threonine detected at relative abundance 13.6 versus alanine = 100; quantum yield \\(1.1\\times10^{-6}\\) | Naphthalene, H\u2082O, NH\u2083 + UV/EUV | **Direct detection**, but extremely low absolute conversion. |\n| Interstellar lactaldehyde synthesis | Lactaldehyde made from CO + ethanol ice | CO, C\u2082H\u2085OH + energetic electrons | **Upstream precursor only**; strongly relevant because lactaldehyde is the required C\u2083 aldehyde for Strecker threonine. |\n| Aldehyde/ammonia \u201cformose-like\u201d parent-body chemistry | Many hydroxy amino acids, but **not threonine/allo-threonine** except trace L-Thr attributed to contamination | HCHO, CH\u2083CHO, NH\u2083, H\u2082O, \u00b1 glycolaldehyde | **Important negative result** for threonine. |\n| \u201cComplex glycine precursor\u201d alkylation | Acetaldehyde proposed as the side-chain donor for Thr | Irradiation-derived glycine precursor + CH\u2083CHO | **Proposed, not demonstrated threonine synthesis**; currently speculative. |\n\nTwo distinctions are crucial for synthesis-network design. First, **\u201cthreonine detected after 6 M HCl hydrolysis\u201d is not equivalent to \u201cfree threonine formed in the prebiotic reactor.\u201d** Several HCN, radiation, and ice experiments instead generate nitriles, oligomers, or other bound precursors that release threonine analytically. Second, the best-defined carbon-skeleton chemistry converges repeatedly on **acetaldehyde, lactaldehyde, glycine, or HCN oligomers**, making these more useful nodes for a reaction-network model than \u201cthreonine formation\u201d treated as a single black-box edge.\n\n## Known synthesis routes and their mechanisms\n\n### Cyanosulfidic photoredox chemistry through lactaldehyde\n\nThe most mechanistically explicit modern route was developed by **Dougal Ritson and John Sutherland (2013, *Angewandte Chemie International Edition*)** and incorporated into the broader cyanosulfidic network by **Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*)**. Its central idea is a photochemical, reductive version of Kiliani\u2013Fischer homologation in which an HCN-derived nitrile is reduced to an aldehyde, the aldehyde adds another HCN unit, and the new cyanohydrin is reduced again.\n\nFor threonine, the chemically important branch is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CHO + HCN \\rightarrow CH_3CH(OH)CN}\n\\]\n\nacetaldehyde \u2192 **lactonitrile**, followed by photoreductive conversion of the nitrile carbon to an aldehyde:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CN \\rightarrow CH_3CH(OH)CHO}\n\\]\n\ngiving **lactaldehyde (2-hydroxypropanal)**. Lactaldehyde then enters ordinary Strecker chemistry:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CHO + HCN + NH_3\n\\rightleftharpoons\nCH_3CH(OH)CH(NH_2)CN}\n\\]\n\nfollowed, in principle, by nitrile hydrolysis to:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{CH_3CH(OH)CH(NH_2)COOH}\n\\]\n\nwhich is threonine/allothreonine. Ritson and Sutherland explicitly obtained lactaldehyde from the acetaldehyde-cyanohydrin system in **19% yield after 2 h**, and subsequently showed that addition of HCN and excess NH\u2083 converted lactaldehyde through its cyanohydrin into the **(allo-)threonine Strecker aminonitrile**. Conversion of related aldehydes to aminonitriles proceeded over days to weeks.\n\nThe underlying photoreduction was demonstrated under approximately neutral aqueous conditions. In a representative first-stage experiment, Ritson and Sutherland used **10 mM glycolonitrile, 30 mM H\u2082S, 33 mM phosphate, 10 mol% CuCN, pH 7, and 254-nm irradiation for 2 h**, obtaining glycolaldehyde hydrate in **42% yield**. The same photochemical logic generated acetaldehyde and ultimately lactaldehyde. H\u2082S acts as a stoichiometric reductant while UV excitation produces the reducing chemistry; Cu(I) greatly facilitates the original photoredox implementation.\n\nPatel et al. subsequently showed that this chemistry belongs to a larger network in which HCN-derived carbon can furnish precursors of ribonucleotides, amino acids and lipids. Importantly, in H\u2082S-containing versions, Cu(I) is not always indispensable to reduction, so copper should be regarded as a demonstrated accelerator/photoredox component rather than an invariant stoichiometric requirement of every version of the network.\n\nA later alternative photoredox system was demonstrated by **Xu, Ritson, Ranjan, Todd, Sasselov and Sutherland (2018, *Chemical Communications*)**: UV irradiation of **ferrocyanide plus sulfite** efficiently drives HCN reductive homologation to simple sugars and amino-acid/hydroxy-acid precursors. This increases the geochemical flexibility of the reductive-homologation module, although Xu et al. did **not establish a new threonine endpoint comparable to Ritson and Sutherland's lactaldehyde/aminonitrile experiment**.\n\nThe strengths of this route are unusually clear intermediates, a measured lactaldehyde yield, aqueous conditions near neutral pH, and a common chemical network with other classes of prebiotic molecules. Its weaknesses are equally clear: the endpoint experimentally emphasized is the **aminonitrile precursor**, not an isolated yield of free threonine; the route requires coordinated supplies of cyanide, reductant, UV, ammonia and sometimes copper; and there are geochemical tensions between abundant sulfide and UV transmission, discussed below.\n\n### Aminomalononitrile plus acetaldehyde\n\nA chemically distinct HCN route was demonstrated by **John W. Thanassi (1975, *Journal of Molecular Evolution*)**. Aminomalononitrile, NH\u2082CH(CN)\u2082, is the **HCN trimer**. Thanassi found that it reacts with electrophiles, including aldehydes, under mild aqueous conditions; acid hydrolysis of the products yielded several amino acids, explicitly including **D,L-threonine and allo-threonine**.\n\nFor threonine the electrophile is **acetaldehyde**. The reaction can be represented schematically as nucleophilic C\u2013C bond formation at the activated carbon of aminomalononitrile:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NH_2CH(CN)_2 + CH_3CHO\n\\rightarrow\nNH_2C(CN)_2CH(OH)CH_3}\n\\]\n\nfollowed by conversion of both nitriles into carboxyl groups under acid hydrolysis and loss of one carboxyl group by decarboxylation:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{NH_2C(COOH)_2CH(OH)CH_3\n\\rightarrow\nNH_2CH(COOH)CH(OH)CH_3 + CO_2}\n\\]\n\nA later experimental paper by **Kuroda et al. (2020, *Natural Product Communications*)**, while extending the aminomalononitrile concept to other amino acids, describes Thanassi's threonine reaction as occurring by **alkylation with acetaldehyde under almost neutral aqueous conditions**, followed by acid hydrolysis and decarboxylation. Thanassi's primary abstract reports that the products were quantitatively measured but the numerical threonine yield is not given in the accessible abstract, so it would be unsafe to invent a percentage.\n\nNo mineral or metal catalyst is required for the demonstrated C\u2013C-forming step. The route therefore has an attractive minimality: **HCN \u2192 HCN trimer; HCN trimer + acetaldehyde \u2192 threonine precursor; hydrolysis/decarboxylation \u2192 threonine**. Its main liabilities are upstream. It requires enough HCN to generate and retain aminomalononitrile, acetaldehyde must be available simultaneously, and the laboratory endpoint uses strong acid hydrolysis. It also produces threonine together with its allo stereoisomer rather than solving threonine's stereochemical-selection problem.\n\nFor network modeling, aminomalononitrile is particularly interesting because it provides a **C\u2082N\u2083 activated carbon synthon generated directly by HCN oligomerization**, rather than requiring lactaldehyde. It therefore constitutes a genuinely independent branch from the lactaldehyde/Strecker route even though both ultimately depend on cyanide chemistry.\n\n### Condensation of glycine with acetaldehyde\n\nA much less frequently discussed but experimentally important route was reported by **C. P. Ivanov and O. C. Ivanov (1983, *Origins of Life*)**. They reacted **glycine and glycine oligohomopeptides with formaldehyde and acetaldehyde** in homogeneous weakly acidic solution. The reactions were run at **pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C, explicitly in the absence of inorganic solids**. Among the products were the expected serine and **threonine**, together with alanine, glutamate, aspartate, norvaline, isoleucine and nonprotein hydroxy amino acids.\n\nThe simplest net threonine transformation is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{glycine + acetaldehyde \\rightarrow threonine/allothreonine}\n\\]\n\nThat C\u2013C connectivity is the reverse of the familiar retro-aldol cleavage of threonine to glycine plus acetaldehyde. The prebiotic experiment is especially significant because it does **not require cyanide in the threonine-forming step, metal minerals, UV or ionizing radiation**. Glycine can in principle be supplied by many other prebiotic routes, so this reaction creates a useful point of convergence between otherwise unrelated origin-of-life networks.\n\nThe trade-off is selectivity. The Ivanovs obtained a fairly broad amino-acid inventory, indicating that aldehyde\u2013amino-acid chemistry in this regime is not a selective threonine synthesis. The accessible primary abstract gives no isolated threonine percentage or threonine:allothreonine ratio. More fundamentally, the route moves rather than eliminates the precursor problem: it requires a pool containing **glycine or oligoglycine plus acetaldehyde at 60\u201380 \u00b0C**.\n\nNevertheless, among routes relevant to network design it deserves more attention than it commonly receives because it provides a direct edge:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{glycine}+\\text{acetaldehyde}\\rightarrow\\text{threonine-family products}}\n\\]\n\nunder catalyst-free aqueous conditions.\n\n### Bulk HCN and ammonia chemistry\n\nThreonine also appears in the older literature on **HCN oligomerization/polymerization**, where the immediate molecular precursor is much less well defined.\n\n**Lowe, Rees and Markham (1963, *Nature*)** demonstrated that ammonium cyanide produces a complex collection of amino acids, amino-acid polymers, fatty acids and purines. Closely related work by **Clifford Matthews and Robert Moser (1966\u20131967, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences* and *Nature*)** showed that HCN-derived polymers and HCN/ammonia reaction products can, after hydrolysis, yield a broad suite of \u03b1-amino acids. Matthews and Moser's hydrolysates explicitly contained **threonine**, alongside glycine, serine, aspartate, histidine, lysine and other amino acids.\n\nThe key mechanistic point is that this is **not clean Strecker chemistry from a known carbonyl**. HCN produces dimers, trimers, tetramers and higher nitrogen-rich oligomers; water and ammonia then transform these networks into amide-, peptide- and amino-acid-like structures. Matthews and Moser emphasized peptide-like material that released amino acids only after strong hydrolysis. Thus the experiment proves that an HCN/NH\u2083 network can encode the threonine carbon skeleton, but does not identify a single dominant elementary pathway to it.\n\nThis ambiguity is important. A reactor that yields threonine only after **6 N HCl at approximately 100 \u00b0C for many hours** has demonstrated a hydrolyzable threonine precursor, not necessarily an environmental pool of free threonine. Moreover, classical HCN-polymer experiments commonly use very high cyanide concentrations and alkaline/ammoniacal conditions, making concentration a major prebiotic-plausibility issue. The chemistry is real; its geochemical deployment remains contested.\n\n### Ionizing-radiation chemistry of HCN\n\n**Sweeney, Toste and Ponnamperuma (1976, *Origins of Life*)** exposed **0.004\u20130.1 M aqueous HCN** to ^60Co \u03b3-radiation. After irradiation and hydrolysis they identified seven protein amino acids by chromatographic/mass-spectrometric methods: glycine, alanine, valine, serine, **threonine**, aspartate and glutamate. The reported ^60Co dose rate was approximately **200,000 rad h\u207b\u00b9**, equivalent to about **2 kGy h\u207b\u00b9**. Control experiments were used to exclude contamination.\n\n**Zorica Dragani\u0107, Ivan Dragani\u0107, Akira Shimoyama and Cyril Ponnamperuma (1977, *Origins of Life*)** subsequently irradiated oxygen-free aqueous HCN, NH\u2084CN and NaCN and concluded that the process is initiated by **free-radical chemistry producing oligomeric products that release amino acids on hydrolysis**. Chiral products were approximately racemic, and radiation-chemical amino-acid yields varied with radiation dose.\n\nThis route needs no mineral catalyst: the source of activation is ionizing radiation and water-derived/cyanide-derived radical chemistry. Its principal prebiotic uncertainty is therefore not chemical feasibility but **dosimetry**. A ^60Co source is a laboratory proxy, not a natural Hadean source; demonstrating that an equivalent dose can be supplied at useful rates by radionuclides, energetic particles, impact radiation or another natural process remains necessary before the laboratory route can be promoted to a quantitatively compelling terrestrial scenario. The analytical-hydrolysis caveat also applies: threonine was recovered after hydrolysis of irradiated products.\n\n### Electric-discharge synthesis\n\nThe best direct atmospheric-discharge evidence comes from the archived 1958 experiment reanalyzed by **Eric Parker and colleagues (2011, *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences*)**. Miller had charged a 5-L apparatus with approximately **258 mmHg CH\u2084, 100 mmHg H\u2082S, 250 mmHg NH\u2083 and 87 mmHg CO\u2082**, then subjected the recirculating system to electrical discharge. Modern UPLC and time-of-flight MS analysis found **23 amino acids and four amines**. Threonine, leucine and isoleucine were detected in this H\u2082S-rich experiment but not in the comparison classic, volcanic or silent-discharge sample sets examined by the same group.\n\nNo intentional mineral catalyst was present. However, Parker et al. noted sulfur deposition near the **tungsten electrodes**, leaving open the possibility that electrode/sulfur surface chemistry modified product distributions. Consequently, it is safest to describe the experiment as **spark-driven rather than rigorously catalyst-free**. The detailed pathway to threonine was not established; plausible chemistry includes formation of carbonyls, HCN and nitriles followed by aqueous reactions, but assigning a particular threonine mechanism would go beyond the experiment.\n\nThe result is nevertheless important because it demonstrates that threonine can emerge from a complex gas-phase/aqueous reaction network without supplying lactaldehyde or aminomalononitrile by hand. The drawback is environmental. The experiment is strongly reducing and unusually rich in CH\u2084, NH\u2083 and H\u2082S compared with most models of the global Hadean atmosphere. Parker et al. themselves therefore emphasized possible **local or transient reducing environments**, including volcanic settings, rather than requiring such a composition globally.\n\nA separate historical line of work by **Matthews and Moser (1966, *PNAS*)** subjected dry CH\u2084/NH\u2083 mixtures to electrical discharge and found threonine among amino acids released by hydrolysis of resulting peptide-like residues. This configuration differs chemically from the canonical Miller water-circulation experiment and again points to a complex hydrolyzable precursor network rather than a uniquely resolved threonine-forming elementary reaction.\n\n### Extraterrestrial ice photochemistry\n\nAstrophysical ice chemistry provides two separate direct threonine demonstrations.\n\n**Jamie Elsila, Jason Dworkin, Max Bernstein, Mildred Martin and Scott Sandford (2007, *The Astrophysical Journal*)** irradiated interstellar-ice analogues at approximately **20 K**. In an experiment replacing methanol with ethanol, the starting ice had composition:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{H_2O:C_2H_5OH:HCN:NH_3=20:2:1:1}.\n\\]\n\nThe overall amino-acid yield was lower than in the corresponding methanol system: glycine was only about **10% of the glycine yield in the methanol-containing ice**. But the carbon-chain diversity increased substantially. In the hydrolyzed residue, relative abundances normalized to glycine = 100 included **D,L-threonine = 60 and D,L-allothreonine = 30**, along with alanine = 26, \u03b2-alanine = 5, serine = 14 and several other products.\n\nThis is one of the strongest quantitative threonine results in the entire literature. It is also mechanistically sobering. Isotope-labeling experiments in the same study showed that amino-acid formation in UV-processed ices proceeds through **multiple pathways**, and for the compounds investigated in detail the dominant routes did not conform simply to either a textbook Strecker mechanism or the simplest radical\u2013radical scheme. Threonine's own detailed atom-by-atom pathway was not resolved.\n\nA second experiment by **Y.-J. Chen, Michel Nuevo and colleagues (2008, *Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society*)** irradiated a **1:1:1 naphthalene:H\u2082O:NH\u2083 ice at 15 K** with **4\u201320 eV photons**. Thirteen amino acids were identified. Alanine was most abundant at an extrapolated **2.2 nmol** in the entire sample; threonine had a relative abundance of **13.6 when alanine = 100** and an estimated photochemical quantum yield of **\\(1.1\\times10^{-6}\\)**.\n\nThe naphthalene experiment is relevant to broad surveys because it demonstrates a threonine route that does **not begin with HCN as the supplied carbon source**. Its limitation is efficiency: only a tiny fraction of the PAH carbon entered identified amino acids, and many other organic photoproducts dominated the residue. The authors therefore interpreted it as evidence for multiple possible astrochemical mechanisms, not a selective threonine factory.\n\n### Interstellar production of the critical lactaldehyde precursor\n\nA major advance relevant to the Strecker route came from **Wang et al. (2024, *Nature Communications*)**, who directly synthesized **lactaldehyde** in an interstellar-ice analogue. A CO/ethanol ice at **5 K** was exposed to energetic electrons as proxies for galactic cosmic rays. The experimentally supported elementary step is radical\u2013radical combination:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HCO^\\bullet + CH_3CHOH^\\bullet\n\\rightarrow CH_3CH(OH)CHO}\n\\]\n\nthat is, formyl + 1-hydroxyethyl \u2192 lactaldehyde. Because radical\u2013radical recombination is barrierless once the partners meet, the chemistry is viable at extremely low temperature. Lactaldehyde was identified using isomer-selective photoionization mass spectrometry and isotopic substitution.\n\nThis experiment does **not make threonine**, but it solves an important upstream problem independently of terrestrial cyanosulfidic chemistry. Lactaldehyde made in molecular-cloud/protoplanetary ice could in principle be incorporated into asteroids or comets, undergo aqueous Strecker chemistry with HCN/NH\u2083 during parent-body alteration, or be delivered to an early planetary surface. Thus two conceptually different frameworks now converge on the same high-value network node, lactaldehyde:\n\n\\[\n\\text{surface HCN photochemistry} \\rightarrow \\text{lactaldehyde}\n\\]\n\nand\n\n\\[\n\\text{CO/ethanol interstellar ice chemistry} \\rightarrow \\text{lactaldehyde}.\n\\]\n\n### Proposed complex-glycine-precursor route\n\n**Kuroda and Kobayashi (2024, *Molecules*)** proposed a broader \u201ccomplex glycine precursor\u201d or CGP framework in which energetic processing first creates a polymeric/complex glycine precursor and subsequent side-chain alkylation generates proteinogenic amino-acid skeletons. In their threonine scheme, **acetaldehyde is the proposed side-chain precursor**. The authors discuss hydrogen bonding as a possible source of preference for threonine over allothreonine.\n\nThis route must presently be kept separate from experimentally established threonine syntheses. The authors themselves identify kinetic and mechanistic uncertainties, and a complete CGP \u2192 threonine transformation with measured yield was not demonstrated. It is therefore useful as a **network hypothesis**, particularly because it again makes acetaldehyde a central C\u2082 precursor, but it should not be assigned the evidentiary weight of the Thanassi, Ivanov, Ritson\u2013Sutherland, Parker or ice experiments.\n\n## Intermediates, catalysts, and quantitative experimental evidence\n\nAcross otherwise very different frameworks, only a relatively small number of intermediates repeatedly matter.\n\n**Acetaldehyde (CH\u2083CHO)** is the most conspicuous hub. It supplies the methyl-bearing carbon unit in the aminomalononitrile reaction, reacts with HCN to produce lactonitrile in the cyanosulfidic route, reacts directly with glycine/oligoglycine in the Ivanov route, and is the proposed side-chain reagent in the CGP hypothesis. It also occurs naturally among products of energetic prebiotic chemistry.\n\n**Lactonitrile, CH\u2083CH(OH)CN**, is the key reduced-homologation intermediate linking acetaldehyde to lactaldehyde. **Lactaldehyde, CH\u2083CH(OH)CHO**, is then the direct carbonyl precursor required by a conventional Strecker construction of the threonine skeleton. The final immediate Strecker precursor is **2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile**, whose nitrile carbon becomes threonine's carboxyl carbon upon hydrolysis.\n\n**Aminomalononitrile, NH\u2082CH(CN)\u2082**, provides an alternative HCN-derived route in which the amino functionality and activated \u03b1-carbon are already present before acetaldehyde addition. This route bypasses lactaldehyde altogether.\n\n**Glycine** is the minimal amino-acid precursor in the Ivanov chemistry. Since glycine itself is among the easiest amino acids to obtain from discharge, HCN, Strecker and extraterrestrial chemistry, the glycine + acetaldehyde reaction could potentially act as a downstream convergence step rather than requiring a unique dedicated route from inorganic gases to threonine.\n\nA compact comparison of experimentally imposed conditions illustrates how different the routes actually are:\n\n| Study | Reaction conditions | Catalyst/energy | Threonine-specific outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Ritson & Sutherland 2013, Angew. Chem. Int. Ed.** | Representative photoreduction: ~10 mM nitrile, 30 mM H\u2082S, 33 mM phosphate, pH 7, 254 nm; CuCN ~10 mol%; 2 h | UV + H\u2082S; Cu(I) photoredox | Lactaldehyde **19%**; subsequent HCN/NH\u2083 gave Thr/allo-Thr aminonitrile. |\n| **Thanassi 1975, J. Mol. Evol.** | Mild, approximately neutral aqueous reaction of aminomalononitrile with acetaldehyde; subsequent acid hydrolysis/decarboxylation | **No mineral catalyst required** | D,L-threonine + allo-threonine; paper states products were quantified, but accessible abstract does not expose numeric yield. |\n| **Ivanov & Ivanov 1983, Origins of Life** | pH **3.3\u20133.7**, **60\u201380 \u00b0C**, glycine/oligoglycine + HCHO/CH\u2083CHO | Explicitly **no inorganic solids** | Threonine among several protein and nonprotein amino acids. |\n| **Sweeney, Toste & Ponnamperuma 1976, Origins of Life** | HCN **0.004\u20130.1 M**, ^60Co irradiation, about **200,000 rad h\u207b\u00b9** | \u03b3-radiation; no conventional catalyst | Thr among seven protein amino acids after hydrolysis. |\n| **Parker et al. 2011, PNAS** | CH\u2084/H\u2082S/NH\u2083/CO\u2082 \u2248 258/100/250/87 mmHg in Miller apparatus | Electrical spark; tungsten electrodes | Thr detected; absent from three comparison Miller sample sets. |\n| **Elsila et al. 2007, ApJ** | H\u2082O:C\u2082H\u2085OH:HCN:NH\u2083 = **20:2:1:1**, ~20 K | UV photolysis of ice | Hydrolysate: Gly = 100, Thr = **60**, allo-Thr = **30**. |\n| **Chen et al. 2008, MNRAS** | C\u2081\u2080H\u2088:H\u2082O:NH\u2083 = **1:1:1**, 15 K, 4\u201320 eV photons | UV/EUV | Thr relative abundance **13.6** vs Ala = 100; quantum yield **1.1\u00d710\u207b\u2076**. |\n| **Wang et al. 2024, Nat. Commun.** | CO/ethanol ice, **5 K**, electron irradiation | Energetic electrons/cosmic-ray proxy | Lactaldehyde, not Thr; barrierless HCO\u2022 + CH\u2083CHOH\u2022 recombination. |\n\nSeveral conclusions follow from the catalyst literature.\n\nThere is **no universal catalytic requirement** for threonine chemistry. Aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde and glycine/acetaldehyde reactions work without minerals; \u03b3-radiolysis and astrophysical-ice routes are energy-driven; discharge chemistry likewise does not require an intentionally added mineral.\n\nCopper has the clearest demonstrated catalytic role in the threonine-specific modern surface chemistry. CuCN/Cu(I) participates in photoredox cycling that accelerates nitrile reductions in the Ritson\u2013Sutherland system. However, H\u2082S-driven versions can function without Cu(I), and **sulfite/ferrocyanide** offers a distinct photoredox implementation of HCN reductive homologation. It would therefore be incorrect to encode \u201cCuCN required\u201d as an absolute dependency for every HCN-to-hydroxyaldehyde network.\n\nNo clay, FeS, NiS or alkaline-hydrothermal mineral has yet emerged from the threonine-specific literature as an experimentally established catalyst comparable to copper in the photoredox route. Generic amino-acid syntheses on minerals or in hydrothermal systems exist, but that is not equivalent to demonstrating threonine formation; the target-specific evidence surveyed here does not justify adding a \u201chydrothermal mineral \u2192 threonine\u201d edge merely by analogy.\n\nA final analytical caution is essential. **Strong acid hydrolysis is itself a chemical transformation.** In HCN-polymer, radiolysis, and several ice experiments, it converts nitriles, amides or oligomer-bound precursors into measurable amino acids. Such experiments establish that the irradiation or polymerization network contained the appropriate carbon\u2013nitrogen framework, but not necessarily that free threonine accumulated before laboratory hydrolysis. Dragani\u0107 et al.'s finding that irradiated cyanide gives oligomers that release amino acids is particularly explicit on this point.\n\n## Environmental contexts and geochemical plausibility\n\n### Sunlit surface waters, ponds, and evaporative basins\n\nOf the terrestrial environments, shallow surface waters are the closest fit to the Ritson\u2013Sutherland chemistry because the reactions need UV radiation, liquid water, cyanide and a reducing sulfur species. The difficult issue is not whether the organic reactions work but whether all necessary reagents can coexist at useful concentrations.\n\nThere is meaningful geochemical support for concentrating cyanide. **Jonathan Toner and David Catling (2019, *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta*)** modeled closed-basin alkaline lakes and experimentally constrained precipitation of **sodium ferrocyanide salts** over plausible early-Earth conditions. Evaporation and mineral weathering can stockpile atmospheric HCN as ferrocyanide; subsequent heating of sediments can release concentrated cyanide salts. This provides a concrete geological mechanism for overcoming at least part of the \u201cdilute HCN\u201d problem.\n\nThe sulfide side is more contentious. **Sukrit Ranjan, Zoe Todd, John Sutherland and Dimitar Sasselov (2018, *Astrobiology*)** calculated early-Earth sulfide speciation and concluded that substantial aqueous HS\u207b concentrations are generally difficult to maintain because metal ions precipitate sulfides and because atmospheric sulfur species affect UV transmission. They explicitly identified a possible tension in cyanosulfidic scenarios: H\u2082S/SO\u2082 can provide sulfur-derived reducing species, but abundant sulfur gases/aerosols can also attenuate the UV needed to drive photochemistry. The authors did identify local geochemical circumstances\u2014and sulfite/bisulfite chemistry\u2014that can partly alleviate the problem, so this is a constraint rather than a disproof.\n\nAccordingly, a **localized, periodically concentrated surface setting** is better supported than a homogeneous \u201cprimordial ocean\u201d for this route. Evaporation, cyanide storage, water-level cycling and spatial separation could provide concentrations that open-ocean chemistry cannot. But no single field site or geochemical model has yet demonstrated simultaneous natural availability at laboratory-effective levels of HCN, ammonia, sulfur reductant, phosphate and photoredox catalyst.\n\n### Reducing atmospheres and volcanic environments\n\nThe Miller H\u2082S experiment establishes excellent **chemical possibility** but weaker global environmental plausibility. Modern discussions generally favor an early atmosphere substantially richer in N\u2082 and CO\u2082 than the highly reducing CH\u2084/NH\u2083 atmosphere used in classic Miller chemistry. **Cleaves, Chalmers, Lazcano, Miller and Bada (2008, *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*)** nevertheless showed that even neutral N\u2082/CO\u2082 discharge mixtures can make significant amino acids if nitrite/nitrate-mediated destruction during workup is suppressed, for example by ferrous iron. Their result weakens the simplistic claim that atmospheric amino-acid synthesis requires a permanently methane-rich planet, although it did not specifically establish threonine production from neutral gas mixtures.\n\nFor threonine itself, the strongest discharge result remains Parker et al.'s H\u2082S-rich experiment, whose CH\u2084/NH\u2083/H\u2082S inventory is more plausibly treated as **local or transient**\u2014for example around reducing volcanic plumes\u2014than as a global atmospheric composition.\n\nImpact-generated reducing atmospheres provide another way to generate temporary H\u2082/CH\u2084/HCN-rich conditions. Modern atmospheric modeling finds that large impacts can produce reducing episodes lasting roughly **10\u2075\u201310\u2077 years**, with HCN production rising sharply when CH\u2084/CO\u2082 becomes sufficiently high. Such models do not synthesize threonine directly, but they provide a plausible upstream feedstock source for the HCN-dependent threonine routes.\n\nThus the atmospheric framework is best viewed as a **feedstock generator feeding aqueous chemistry**, rather than requiring threonine itself to be completed in the gas phase.\n\n### Warm acidic waters\n\nThe Ivanov glycine\u2013acetaldehyde reaction at pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C is chemically compatible with warm acidic pools, volcanic-lake margins or hydrothermally warmed surface waters. Unlike the cyanosulfidic pathway, it does not require UV or a metal catalyst during the threonine-forming step.\n\nIts environmental weakness is quantitative rather than qualitative: no geochemical model accompanying the original experiment showed that glycine, glycine oligomers and acetaldehyde would naturally reach the concentrations and residence times needed together. Since the reaction generated numerous amino acids rather than selectively accumulating threonine, concentrating and preserving the product is another unresolved problem.\n\n### Hydrothermal vents\n\nThe literature does **not** presently make high-temperature submarine hydrothermal vents the strongest threonine-specific setting. Although generic amino-acid syntheses have been investigated under hydrothermal conditions, target-specific evidence for efficient threonine synthesis is weak, while experimental degradation data point in the opposite direction.\n\nA 2005 *Chemistry Letters* experimental study, **\u201cThreonine Transformation under Hydrothermal Conditions,\u201d** heated threonine solutions between **121 and 160 \u00b0C** and observed conversion to glycine together with other products, including peptide/polymeric material. Thus temperatures characteristic of many vent reactors can destroy or transform threonine on geologically relevant residence times.\n\nThis does not exclude cooler hydrothermal gradients, mineral pores or low-temperature vent margins. It does mean that a network model should not simply equate \u201chydrothermal amino-acid synthesis\u201d with \u201cthreonine production\u201d; **formation and destruction must be modeled simultaneously**, and high-temperature exposure may act as a sink rather than a source for threonine.\n\n### Meteorite parent bodies and interstellar ices\n\nExtraterrestrial chemistry has unusually strong direct experimental support for assembling the threonine skeleton. The Elsila ethanol-ice experiment actually produces substantial threonine relative to glycine in the hydrolyzed residue, while Chen et al. independently produce threonine from PAH/H\u2082O/NH\u2083 ice. Wang et al. add a mechanistically clean route to lactaldehyde at 5 K.\n\nWhat is less secure is the transfer from laboratory ice residue to a meaningful early-Earth inventory. Irradiation doses, survival during ice warming and planetesimal processing, conversion of bound precursor into free amino acid, delivery rates and post-delivery destruction all have to be considered. Elsila et al.'s isotope experiments also caution against assigning simple textbook mechanisms to these residues: several pathways operate simultaneously.\n\nMeteorite observations do not currently provide unambiguous corroboration specifically for indigenous threonine. **Koga and Naraoka (2017, *Scientific Reports*)** detected many hydroxy amino acids in Murchison, but the observed L-enrichment of proteinogenic compounds\u2014including threonine\u2014coincided with proteinogenic amino acids in procedural blanks and was judged potentially attributable to terrestrial contamination. In their complementary aldehyde/NH\u2083 experiment, **threonine and allothreonine were not produced**, apart from a small amount of L-threonine interpreted as likely contamination.\n\nThat is an important negative constraint. The experiment used H\u2082O/NH\u2083/HCHO/CH\u2083CHO at a **1000:100:10:1 molar ratio**, with or without glycolaldehyde, heated at **60 \u00b0C for six days** under N\u2082. It successfully generated many other hydroxy amino acids, showing that \u201caldehydes + ammonia + aqueous parent-body alteration\u201d is not automatically sufficient to make threonine.\n\nConsequently, extraterrestrial threonine is chemically plausible and experimentally producible, but **meteorite occurrence by itself is presently poorer evidence for threonine than for many nonproteinogenic amino acids**, because terrestrial contamination is particularly difficult to exclude for a common biological amino acid.\n\n## Main unresolved questions and published critiques\n\n### The free-threonine versus precursor problem\n\nThis is probably the most important interpretive issue. The cyanosulfidic route very convincingly reaches the **threonine aminonitrile**, but a network designer must still assign kinetics to aminonitrile hydrolysis under realistic environmental pH and temperature. HCN-radiolysis and many ice experiments similarly reveal threonine mainly after laboratory hydrolysis.\n\nThe distinction matters because nitrile hydrolysis is irreversible enough to pull Strecker equilibria toward amino acids, but its uncatalyzed rate can be slow under mild neutral conditions. Strong analytical hydrolysis therefore cannot simply be represented as an instantaneous environmental reaction. A useful synthesis network should distinguish at least:\n\n\\[\n\\text{lactaldehyde}\n\\rightleftharpoons\n\\text{cyanohydrin}\n\\rightleftharpoons\n\\text{aminonitrile}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{threonine}\n\\]\n\nrather than collapsing all four species into one \u201cStrecker synthesis\u201d node. Ritson and Sutherland's observation that aminonitrile formation itself can require **days to weeks** reinforces the kinetic importance of intermediate persistence.\n\n### Cyanide concentration\n\nHCN is versatile but difficult to concentrate in bulk water. The strongest classic HCN-polymer pathways often operate at concentrations far above plausible unconcentrated rainfall or oceanic HCN. Toner and Catling's alkaline-lake/ferrocyanide model is therefore valuable because it provides a physically motivated concentration and storage mechanism, but it introduces its own mineralogical and thermal requirements.\n\nThe cyanosulfidic route is less extreme in substrate concentration than old bulk-HCN polymerization, but it still requires a setting able to supply cyanide repeatedly while preventing excessive loss through volatilization, hydrolysis, metal complexation or competing oligomerization.\n\n### Lactaldehyde availability\n\nFor a clean Strecker route, lactaldehyde is the central bottleneck. This was historically a weak point because hydroxy aldehydes are reactive and not obviously abundant geochemical species. The situation is now substantially better than it was a decade ago: Ritson and Sutherland produced lactaldehyde at **19% yield** by aqueous photoreduction, and Wang et al. independently demonstrated lactaldehyde production in irradiated CO/ethanol ices at 5 K.\n\nWhat remains unknown is whether either source creates environmentally persistent, sufficiently concentrated lactaldehyde before it undergoes oxidation, reduction, aldol chemistry, polymerization or other side reactions. This makes lactaldehyde an especially high-value target for future geochemical lifetime measurements.\n\n### Stereochemical selectivity\n\nNone of the experimentally mature abiotic routes solves the biological stereochemistry problem. Thanassi explicitly obtained **D,L-threonine and allo-threonine**; the Elsila ice experiment produced both D,L-threonine and D,L-allothreonine, at relative abundances 60 and 30 respectively; radiolysis experiments gave approximately racemic chiral amino acids.\n\nThus the problem is actually two-dimensional: enrichment of **threonine versus allothreonine** and enrichment of **L versus D** within the threonine pair. No demonstrated prebiotic synthesis surveyed here simultaneously delivers strong threo diastereoselectivity and L enantioselectivity. Claims that one route is especially \u201cbiological\u201d because it makes proteinogenic threonine should therefore be treated cautiously unless the stereoisomer distribution is reported.\n\n### Product complexity and selectivity\n\nThe cleanest-looking reaction schemes do not necessarily yield clean mixtures. Electrical discharge gives dozens of amino acids and amines; the glycine/aldehyde route gives many protein and nonprotein amino acids; ice irradiation gives a chemically complex organic residue; HCN polymerization generates heterogeneous nitrogen-rich solids and oligomers.\n\nThis matters for origin-of-life network design because **absolute yield is not enough**. Competition for acetaldehyde, HCN, ammonia and reducing equivalents may suppress threonine under a fully connected reaction network even when a binary or ternary model reaction works. Conversely, promiscuous networks may be beneficial if the objective is simultaneous access to many building blocks rather than selective threonine manufacture.\n\n### Compatibility of UV and sulfur chemistry\n\nThis is the most explicit geochemical critique of the cyanosulfidic framework. Ranjan et al. found that aqueous hydrosulfide is difficult to sustain generically because Fe and Cu remove sulfide as minerals, while sulfur-rich atmospheres and aerosols can shield the short-wave UV required by photochemistry. They also showed that sulfite and bisulfite can be more readily supplied in some surface waters, and Xu et al. experimentally demonstrated useful photoredox chemistry with sulfite/ferrocyanide. The route is therefore **contested in environmental implementation, not in laboratory chemistry**.\n\nAn important consequence is that \u201csulfidic surface pond\u201d should not be modeled as a single unconstrained reservoir. Metal sulfide precipitation, sulfur atmospheric opacity, cyanide-metal complexation and water depth all alter the simultaneous availability of light and reductant.\n\n### Atmospheric composition\n\nParker et al.'s H\u2082S experiment is analytically convincing but geochemically nonunique. A global atmosphere with the experiment's very high CH\u2084/NH\u2083/H\u2082S partial pressures is not required\u2014and is difficult to reconcile with many atmosphere models. Neutral-atmosphere discharge experiments demonstrate that organics can still be generated, but the threonine-specific result has not been reproduced at comparable strength under a fully CO\u2082/N\u2082-dominated mixture.\n\nThe defensible interpretation is therefore that discharge chemistry remains viable especially in **local reducing plumes, transient impact-generated atmospheres, or other disequilibrium settings**, while a globally persistent Miller-type atmosphere is not necessary and should not be assumed.\n\n### High-temperature survival\n\nA proposed pathway must produce threonine faster than the environment destroys it. Direct hydrothermal experiments show threonine transforming at **121\u2013160 \u00b0C**, including formation of glycine and other products. This makes hot hydrothermal residence a potentially serious sink.\n\nConsequently, a hydrothermal scenario would be more credible if threonine forms in cooler peripheral regions or is rapidly exported down a thermal gradient. Direct synthesis and decomposition kinetics need to be measured in the same mineral/fluid compositions before high-temperature vents can be ranked alongside surface or extraterrestrial routes.\n\n### The formose/ammonia result argues against assuming \u201cgeneric aldehydes make Thr\u201d\n\nKoga and Naraoka's 2017 experiment is particularly valuable because it falsifies an overly broad assumption. Their aldehyde + NH\u2083 system generated an impressive spectrum of C\u2083/C\u2084 hydroxy amino acids, yet **not threonine or allothreonine** at convincing indigenous levels.\n\nThis means that carbon-counting alone is insufficient. Threonine requires the correct positional relationship:\n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{HO{-}CH(CH_3){-}CH(NH_2){-}COOH}.\n\\]\n\nMany formose/aldol pathways favor alternative \u03b1-, \u03b2- and \u03b3-hydroxy amino-acid skeletons instead. In network terms, the route from generic HCHO/CH\u2083CHO/NH\u2083 chemistry to C\u2084H\u2089NO\u2083 is **not** equivalent to a route to threonine.\n\n### Congruence with biology is currently weak\n\nThe experimentally demonstrated prebiotic routes surveyed here use HCN oligomerization, Strecker aminonitriles, aldehyde\u2013glycine condensation, photoredox homologation, discharge chemistry or radical ice chemistry. None gains strong evidentiary support merely by resembling modern cellular threonine synthesis. The absence of biochemical congruence is not a disproof\u2014modern enzymatic metabolism need not preserve the primordial synthetic route\u2014but it means \u201ccontinuity with extant metabolism\u201d currently provides little leverage for choosing among these threonine scenarios.\n\nA more productive comparison is at the **network-node level**: acetaldehyde, glycine, cyanide, hydroxy aldehydes and amino nitriles are products or substrates shared across several prebiotic frameworks. That convergence is experimentally better supported than any claim that one complete pathway is uniquely ancestral.\n\n## Comparative judgment for synthesis-network design\n\nFor a reaction-network model, the literature supports treating the following edges as experimentally grounded:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{acetaldehyde + HCN \\rightarrow lactonitrile}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{lactonitrile \\xrightarrow[H_2S]{UV,\\;Cu(I)} lactaldehyde}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{lactaldehyde + HCN + NH_3 \\rightarrow Thr/alloThr\\ aminonitrile}}\n\\]\n\nwith the lactaldehyde-forming step reported at **19%** under the Ritson\u2013Sutherland photoredox regime.\n\nA second independently grounded branch is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{HCN \\rightarrow aminomalononitrile}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde\n\\rightarrow hydrolyzable\\ Thr/alloThr\\ precursor}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{hydrolysis + decarboxylation\\rightarrow Thr/alloThr}}\n\\]\n\nwith no mineral catalyst needed for the acetaldehyde addition.\n\nA third is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathrm{glycine/oligoglycine + acetaldehyde\n\\rightarrow Thr + other\\ amino\\ acids}}\n\\]\n\nat pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C without inorganic solids.\n\nThe radiation, discharge and ice experiments should initially be represented as **network-generating reaction ensembles** rather than single elementary reactions because the threonine-forming sequence has not been resolved. Their experimental output constraints are nevertheless useful: threonine is definitely accessible under those conditions.\n\nOn current evidence, the most defensible environmental ranking is not a single winner:\n\n**For mechanistic definition**, the aqueous cyanosulfidic/lactaldehyde route is strongest because individual intermediates and a lactaldehyde yield are known.\n\n**For chemical simplicity of the target-specific C\u2013C-forming step**, aminomalononitrile + acetaldehyde and glycine + acetaldehyde are particularly attractive because they do not require a mineral catalyst.\n\n**For direct experimental production from very simple energetic starting mixtures**, the H\u2082S electrical-discharge experiment is compelling, but the environmental gas composition is contested.\n\n**For exogenous synthesis**, the ethanol/HCN/NH\u2083 ice experiment provides unusually strong relative threonine production, and the independent 2024 lactaldehyde experiment supplies a plausible upstream Strecker precursor. The main uncertainties shift from synthesis to hydrolysis, survival and delivery.\n\n**For hot hydrothermal synthesis**, the threonine-specific case is currently weakest: direct synthesis evidence is limited and measured thermal transformation provides a clear degradation constraint.\n\nThe single most important experimental gaps are therefore quantitative rather than conceptual: threonine/allo-threonine yields for the aminomalononitrile and glycine\u2013acetaldehyde routes under rigorously prebiotic workups; direct aminonitrile-to-free-threonine kinetics at environmentally realistic pH, temperature and ionic strength; lactaldehyde lifetimes in mixed geochemical solutions; effects of minerals on both formation and destruction; absolute rather than relative yields in ice experiments; and full stereoisomer-resolved product distributions. These measurements would determine which of the many chemically possible pathways can carry appreciable flux in an integrated prebiotic reaction network.\n\n## Primary literature cited\n\n**Ritson, D. J. & Sutherland, J. D. (2013), *Angewandte Chemie International Edition*, \u201cSynthesis of Aldehydic Ribonucleotide and Amino Acid Precursors by Photoredox Chemistry.\u201d** Demonstrated HCN-derived photoreductive homologation, 19% lactaldehyde from the acetaldehyde-cyanohydrin system, and conversion to the (allo-)threonine Strecker aminonitrile.\n\n**Patel, B. H.; Percivalle, C.; Ritson, D. J.; Duffy, C. D.; Sutherland, J. D. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, \u201cCommon origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism.\u201d** Embedded threonine-precursor chemistry in a common HCN/H\u2082S/UV reaction network.\n\n**Xu, J.; Ritson, D. J.; Ranjan, S.; Todd, Z. R.; Sasselov, D. D.; Sutherland, J. D. (2018), *Chemical Communications*, \u201cPhotochemical reductive homologation of hydrogen cyanide using sulfite and ferrocyanide.\u201d** Demonstrated an alternative sulfite/ferrocyanide photoredox implementation of HCN homologation.\n\n**Thanassi, J. W. (1975), *Journal of Molecular Evolution* 7, 65\u201373, \u201cReactions of aminomalononitrile with electrophiles.\u201d** Direct experimental source for D,L-threonine and allo-threonine from aminomalononitrile/aldehyde chemistry after hydrolysis.\n\n**Ivanov, C. P. & Ivanov, O. C. (1983), *Origins of Life* 13, 97\u2013108, \u201cA study of the interaction of glycine and its oligohomopeptides with formaldehyde and acetaldehyde under possible primitive earth conditions.\u201d** Demonstrated threonine formation at pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C without inorganic solids.\n\n**Lowe, C. U.; Rees, M. W.; Markham, R. (1963), *Nature* 199, 219\u2013222, \u201cSynthesis of Complex Organic Compounds from Simple Precursors: Formation of Amino-Acids, Amino-Acid Polymers, Fatty Acids and Purines from Ammonium Cyanide.\u201d** Foundational bulk-ammonium-cyanide chemistry.\n\n**Matthews, C. N. & Moser, R. E. (1967), *Nature* 215, 1230\u20131234, \u201cPeptide Synthesis from Hydrogen Cyanide and Water.\u201d** Demonstrated that HCN polymers yield peptide/amino-acid material upon reaction with water; closely related experiments identified threonine in hydrolysates.\n\n**Sweeney, M. A.; Toste, A. P.; Ponnamperuma, C. (1976), *Origins of Life* 7, 187\u2013189, \u201cFormation of amino acids by cobalt-60 irradiation of hydrogen cyanide solutions.\u201d** Demonstrated threonine after \u03b3-irradiation of 0.004\u20130.1 M HCN and hydrolysis.\n\n**Dragani\u0107, Z.; Dragani\u0107, I.; Shimoyama, A.; Ponnamperuma, C. (1977), *Origins of Life* 8, 371\u2013376, \u201cEvidence for amino acids in hydrolysates of compounds formed by ionizing radiations. I. Aqueous solutions of HCN, NH\u2084CN, and NaCN.\u201d** Established radical-initiated cyanide oligomer formation, amino-acid release by hydrolysis and near-racemic products.\n\n**Parker, E. T.; Cleaves, H. J.; Dworkin, J. P.; Glavin, D. P.; Callahan, M.; Aubrey, A.; Lazcano, A.; Bada, J. L.; Joyce, G. F. (2011), *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences* 108, 5526\u20135531, \u201cPrimordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H\u2082S-rich spark discharge experiment.\u201d** Direct threonine detection from H\u2082S/CH\u2084/NH\u2083/CO\u2082 spark chemistry.\n\n**Elsila, J. E.; Dworkin, J. P.; Bernstein, M. P.; Martin, M. P.; Sandford, S. A. (2007), *The Astrophysical Journal* 660, 911\u2013918, \u201cMechanisms of Amino Acid Formation in Interstellar Ice Analogs.\u201d** Ethanol-containing UV ice yielded D,L-threonine = 60 and D,L-allothreonine = 30 relative to glycine = 100.\n\n**Chen, Y.-J.; Nuevo, M.; Yih, T.-S.; Ip, W.-H.; Fung, H.-S.; Cheng, C.-Y.; Tsai, H.-R.; Wu, C.-Y. R. (2008), *Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society* 384, 605\u2013610, \u201cAmino acids produced from the ultraviolet/extreme-ultraviolet irradiation of naphthalene in a H\u2082O+NH\u2083 ice mixture.\u201d** Threonine relative abundance 13.6 versus alanine = 100 and quantum yield \\(1.1\\times10^{-6}\\).\n\n**Wang et al. (2024), *Nature Communications* 15, \u201cInterstellar formation of lactaldehyde, a key intermediate in the methylglyoxal pathway.\u201d** Directly demonstrated lactaldehyde formation by HCO\u2022 + CH\u2083CHOH\u2022 radical recombination in CO/ethanol ice at 5 K.\n\n**Koga, T. & Naraoka, H. (2017), *Scientific Reports* 7, \u201cA new family of extraterrestrial amino acids in the Murchison meteorite.\u201d** Critical negative result: aldehyde/NH\u2083 experiments produced many hydroxy amino acids but not convincing threonine/allo-threonine; meteoritic proteinogenic threonine was vulnerable to a terrestrial-contamination interpretation.\n\n**Koga et al. (2021), *Meteoritics & Planetary Science* 56, \u201cExtraterrestrial hydroxy amino acids in CM and CR carbonaceous chondrites.\u201d** Extended the meteoritic hydroxy-amino-acid inventory and formation analysis.\n\n**Toner, J. D. & Catling, D. C. (2019), *Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta* 260, 124\u2013132, \u201cAlkaline lake settings for concentrated prebiotic cyanide and the origin of life.\u201d** Provides a geochemical mechanism for cyanide concentration/storage through ferrocyanide precipitation in closed-basin alkaline lakes.\n\n**Ranjan, S.; Todd, Z. R.; Sutherland, J. D.; Sasselov, D. D. (2018), *Astrobiology*, \u201cSulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry.\u201d** Quantified the difficulties of sustaining high HS\u207b and the tension between sulfur-rich environments and UV-dependent synthesis while identifying sulfite/bisulfite alternatives.\n\n**Cleaves, H. J.; Chalmers, J. H.; Lazcano, A.; Miller, S. L.; Bada, J. L. (2008), *Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres*, \u201cA reassessment of prebiotic organic synthesis in neutral planetary atmospheres.\u201d** Demonstrated substantial discharge amino-acid production from more neutral atmospheric mixtures when oxidative workup losses are controlled.\n\n**Kuroda et al. (2020), *Natural Product Communications*, \u201cReaction of Aminomalononitrile and Benzylic Compounds as a Plausible Route to Phenylalanine.\u201d** Modern experimental treatment of the aminomalononitrile framework and explicit discussion of Thanassi's near-neutral acetaldehyde \u2192 threonine chemistry.\n\n**Kuroda & Kobayashi (2024), *Molecules* 29, 4403, \u201cAlkylation of Complex Glycine Precursor (CGP) as a Prebiotic Route to 20 Proteinogenic Amino Acids Synthesis.\u201d** Proposed, rather than demonstrated, an acetaldehyde/CGP route to threonine.\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH ===\n\nConsider how well the network aligns with this literature in your evaluation.\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n  