tion, pH, residence time and aminomalononitrile persistence relative to competing oligomerization; provide a physically justified concentration or storage mechanism.\",\n        \"Separate the demonstrated acid-workup conditions from proposed environmental hydrolysis, and test whether hydrolysis/decarboxylation releases threonine before competing destruction.\",\n        \"Annotate which intermediates are experimentally identified and which are mechanistic representations.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.79,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.89\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.87,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.72,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.78,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.82,\n      \"feedback\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker chemistry provides a sound glycinonitrile entry, and Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) support the downstream threonine-forming glycine/acetaldehyde channel in warm weakly acidic solution. Representing rxn_013 as a selected product channel is appropriate; it should not imply a selective elementary reaction or an established stereoisomer ratio. The main completion gap is glycinonitrile hydrolysis under the proposed natural conditions. The final reaction also requires sufficient dissolved acetaldehyde and residence time despite volatility and competing reactions.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify and substantiate glycinonitrile hydrolysis conditions and the compatibility of the resulting solution with rxn_013.\",\n        \"Populate rxn_013 with the experimentally supported pH 3.3\u20133.7 and 60\u201380 \u00b0C window, together with concentrations, duration and an acetaldehyde retention or replenishment mechanism.\",\n        \"Measure threonine formation and loss in realistic mixtures rather than inferring selective conversion from the net equation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.78,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.74,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.92,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.89\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.83,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.89,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.63,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.73,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.77,\n      \"feedback\": \"The sequence from aminomalononitrile through aminomalonic acid to glycine is chemically coherent, and aminomalonic acid decarboxylation provides a sensible connection to the experimentally supported Ivanov threonine-forming channel. However, rxn_014 lacks evidence for useful conversion under the unspecified surface hydrolysis conditions. HCN oligomerization selectivity, intermediate persistence and transfer into the downstream acidic reaction pool remain unresolved. This pathway shares the same downstream experimental support and limitations as the other rxn_013 pathways.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Establish whether aminomalononitrile hydrolysis and subsequent decarboxylation produce recoverable glycine before competing oligomerization or decomposition.\",\n        \"Specify concentration changes, pH transitions and transport losses between HCN processing and the warm acidic glycine\u2013acetaldehyde stage.\",\n        \"Test rxn_013 using the resulting glycine-containing mixture to assess inhibition and competing aldehyde consumption.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.76\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.76,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.78,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.52,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.6,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.67,\n      \"feedback\": \"The historical HCN/NH3 studies, including Matthews and Moser, support threonine recovery from hydrolyzed HCN-derived material. An unresolved reaction ensemble is therefore a legitimate representation of the experimental chemistry. Nevertheless, the edge combines precursor production with the decisive release step, whose environmental effectiveness is not established. The strategy acknowledges this correctly, but the pathway remains conditional rather than demonstrating free-threonine accumulation in a terrestrial surface setting.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Represent the hydrolyzable product pool and its release step separately, or explicitly annotate rxn_016 with distinct precursor-generation and workup conditions.\",\n        \"Specify the concentrated ammoniacal HCN operating window and demonstrate an environmental hydrolysis process rather than substituting warm weak acid for the published strong-acid workup without evidence.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.89\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.57,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.69,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.74,\n      \"feedback\": \"HCN-derived glycine supply and the Ivanov glycine\u2013acetaldehyde channel each have experimental support, making their proposed connection chemically sensible. The supplied evidence does not demonstrate the integrated sequence, however. Glycine recovery from HCN-derived material may depend on analytical hydrolysis, and the compatibility of that complex product mixture with the downstream condensation is untested. This is a plausible composite pathway, not an independently demonstrated complete natural synthesis.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_017\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Distinguish free glycine from hydrolysis-released glycine in rxn_017 and establish environmentally effective release where required.\",\n        \"Test the downstream reaction with realistically recovered HCN-derived material, including residual cyanide, ammonia and oligomers.\",\n        \"Specify transfer, dilution and acetaldehyde availability in the warm acidic receiving environment.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_018\": 0.73\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.73,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.76,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.4,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.57,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.62,\n      \"feedback\": \"Sweeney et al. (1976) and Dragani\u0107 et al. (1977) support threonine detection after irradiation and hydrolysis of aqueous cyanide products. The ensemble representation is appropriate, but neither a terrestrial radiation source and dose budget nor an effective natural release process is specified. The reported laboratory dose rate of approximately 2 kGy/h cannot be assumed for an unspecified surface setting. Experimental accessibility of hydrolyzable threonine precursors is substantially better established than environmental production of free threonine.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_018\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify a natural irradiation source, absorbed dose, dose rate, exposure duration and relevant attenuation, accounting for concurrent product destruction.\",\n        \"Separate irradiated precursor production from hydrolysis, and substantiate the latter under the proposed surface conditions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_019\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.89\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.84,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.83,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.46,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.66,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.7,\n      \"feedback\": \"Radiolytic cyanide chemistry supports glycine-bearing products, and routing recovered glycine into the independently supported nonradiative acetaldehyde reaction is chemically reasonable. This avoids assigning an unsupported radiolytic threonine mechanism. It does not resolve natural dosimetry, precursor hydrolysis or recovery of usable glycine, and the integrated mixture-to-mixture sequence has not been demonstrated. Physical transfer and protection from continued irradiation require specification.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_019\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Quantify the natural dose budget and distinguish free glycine production from glycine released during laboratory hydrolysis.\",\n        \"Specify product capture or export from the irradiated environment and test the receiving warm acidic reaction using the recovered mixture.\",\n        \"Assess acetaldehyde consumption and amino-acid degradation alongside threonine formation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_020\": 0.85\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.85,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.82,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.58,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.82,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.77,\n      \"feedback\": \"Parker et al. (2011) provide direct product-level support for threonine in the specified H2S-rich gas\u2013water discharge chemistry. Treating this as an ensemble, rather than an elementary gas-to-amino-acid mechanism, is justified. The environmental limitation is reproducing a sufficiently reducing local gas mixture with effective discharge and aqueous capture. Possible tungsten